INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (topic_id, subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, source, is_active) VALUES
('753003e2-491e-4b8d-a3f6-ca9ac66b76f0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='753003e2-491e-4b8d-a3f6-ca9ac66b76f0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In the context of reactive dyeing via the nucleophilic substitution mechanism (D-R-Cl), how does the addition of an electron-withdrawing group (EWG) like a nitro group on the heterocyclic ring affect the reaction kinetics and the fiber-dye bond stability under alkaline hydrolysis?', '["It increases the reaction rate but decreases the stability of the dye-fiber bond towards alkaline hydrolysis.", "It decreases the reaction rate but increases the stability of the dye-fiber bond.", "It increases both the reaction rate and the stability of the dye-fiber bond.", "It has no significant effect on the kinetics but drastically improves the exhaustion profile."]'::jsonb, 'It increases the reaction rate but decreases the stability of the dye-fiber bond towards alkaline hydrolysis.', 'Electron-withdrawing groups increase the electrophilicity of the reactive center, accelerating the reaction with cellulose, but they also make the resulting ester/ether bond more susceptible to nucleophilic attack by hydroxyl ions.', 3, 'ai', true),
('753003e2-491e-4b8d-a3f6-ca9ac66b76f0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='753003e2-491e-4b8d-a3f6-ca9ac66b76f0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A disperse dye has a molar extinction coefficient (ε) of 30,000 L·mol⁻¹·cm⁻¹ at 550 nm. If a 100% polyester fabric is dyed to a 2% depth (w/w) and the dye exhibits a partition coefficient (K = C_fiber / C_bath) of 500 at equilibrium, calculate the thermodynamic affinity (Δμ°) at 130°C (403 K). Assume a liquor ratio of 10:1 and R = 8.314 J/mol·K.', '["-20.84 kJ/mol", "-15.42 kJ/mol", "-10.25 kJ/mol", "-5.68 kJ/mol"]'::jsonb, '-20.84 kJ/mol', 'Using the formula Δμ° = -RT ln(K), the calculation -8.314 * 403 * ln(500) yields approximately -20.84 kJ/mol, indicating the spontaneity of the dyeing process.', 3, 'ai', true),
('753003e2-491e-4b8d-a3f6-ca9ac66b76f0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='753003e2-491e-4b8d-a3f6-ca9ac66b76f0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'During the reduction of a Vat dye (Indanthrene) using sodium dithionite (Na2S2O4) in an alkaline medium, the redox potential must be carefully monitored. What is the theoretical requirement for the leuco-compound formation if the dye''s standard reduction potential (E°) is -600 mV?', '["The bath potential must be more negative than -600 mV to ensure complete reduction.", "The bath potential must be exactly 0 mV to prevent over-reduction.", "The bath potential must be more positive than -600 mV to prevent sulfonic group cleavage.", "Redox potential is irrelevant; only the pH controls the keto-enol tautomerism."]'::jsonb, 'The bath potential must be more negative than -600 mV to ensure complete reduction.', 'For a reduction reaction to be spontaneous and complete, the reducing agent must provide a system potential lower (more negative) than the reduction potential of the dye molecule.', 3, 'ai', true),
('753003e2-491e-4b8d-a3f6-ca9ac66b76f0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='753003e2-491e-4b8d-a3f6-ca9ac66b76f0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When dyeing nylon 6,6 with acid dyes, the ‘blocking effect’ is observed when using binary mixtures. Which of the following conditions most likely exacerbates this phenomenon?', '["Using two dyes with significantly different saturation values and high affinity at a low pH.", "Operating at the isoelectric point of the fiber (pH 5.2).", "Adding an inorganic salt like Glauber''s salt used as a leveling agent.", "Using dyes with identical molecular weights and ionic charges."]'::jsonb, 'Using two dyes with significantly different saturation values and high affinity at a low pH.', 'Blocking occurs when dyes compete for limited amine end-groups; a high-affinity dye will occupy sites preferentially, ''blocking'' the second dye particularly when sites are limited at low pH.', 3, 'ai', true),
('753003e2-491e-4b8d-a3f6-ca9ac66b76f0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='753003e2-491e-4b8d-a3f6-ca9ac66b76f0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Regarding the aggregation of Direct dyes in aqueous salt solutions, how does the Increase in electrolyte concentration (NaCl) affect the entropy (ΔS) and enthalpy (ΔH) of the system according to the Gouy-Chapman model?', '["It reduces the electrostatic repulsion, decreasing \u0394H (more exothermic) and decreasing \u0394S due to ordered aggregate formation.", "It increases the zeta potential, making \u0394H more positive.", "It increases \u0394S by liberating water molecules from the hydration shell of the dye molecules.", "It has no effect on \u0394H but increases the solubility limit via the common ion effect."]'::jsonb, 'It reduces the electrostatic repulsion, decreasing ΔH (more exothermic) and decreasing ΔS due to ordered aggregate formation.', 'Electrolytes shield the negative charges on direct dyes, allowing Van der Waals forces to dominate (exothermic) while the formation of large, ordered aggregates reduces the system''s entropy.', 3, 'ai', true),
('753003e2-491e-4b8d-a3f6-ca9ac66b76f0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='753003e2-491e-4b8d-a3f6-ca9ac66b76f0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In the Bifunctional Reactive dye chemistry (e.g., Sumifix Supra), one vinyl sulfone and one MCT (Monochlorotriazine) group are present. Why is the fixation yield higher than homo-bifunctional dyes across a wider temperature range?', '["The two groups have different activation energies, providing an ''optimal'' reaction rate at both 40\u00b0C and 80\u00b0C.", "The vinyl sulfone group acts as a catalyst for the MCT group''s nucleophilic substitution.", "They form a unique covalent cross-link between two different cellulose polymer chains.", "The MCT group increases the solubility, preventing the vinyl sulfone from hydrolyzing."]'::jsonb, 'The two groups have different activation energies, providing an ''optimal'' reaction rate at both 40°C and 80°C.', 'Hetero-bifunctional dyes utilize the high reactivity of vinyl sulfone at lower temperatures and the stability/reactivity of MCT at higher temperatures, broadening the efficient dyeing window.', 3, 'ai', true),
('bfd6829f-3d56-4982-a82f-b3e782875fa8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='bfd6829f-3d56-4982-a82f-b3e782875fa8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a high-temperature high-pressure (HTHP) jet dyeing machine, the ''impact zone'' pressure is increased to enhance penetration. If the nozzle diameter is halved while maintaining the same pump pressure, how does the kinetic energy of the liquor per unit mass theoretically change?', '["It remains the same", "It increases by a factor of 4", "It increases by a factor of 16", "It decreases by a factor of 2"]'::jsonb, 'It increases by a factor of 16', 'According to the continuity equation and Bernoulli''s principle, halving the diameter reduces the area by 4, which quadruples velocity; since kinetic energy is proportional to the square of velocity (v^2), the energy increases by 4^2 = 16.', 3, 'ai', true),
('bfd6829f-3d56-4982-a82f-b3e782875fa8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='bfd6829f-3d56-4982-a82f-b3e782875fa8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A jigger dyeing machine is operating at a constant linear speed of 80 m/min. If the initial cloth roll diameter is 0.4m and the final is 1.2m, what must be the ratio of the motor RPM at the start of the batch versus the end of the batch for the take-up roller?', '["1:1", "3:1", "1:3", "9:1"]'::jsonb, '3:1', 'To maintain a constant linear speed (v = ωr), the rotation speed must be inversely proportional to the radius; thus, the ratio is 1.2/0.4 = 3.', 3, 'ai', true),
('bfd6829f-3d56-4982-a82f-b3e782875fa8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='bfd6829f-3d56-4982-a82f-b3e782875fa8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a package dyeing machine, a ''flow reversal'' cycle is set to 6 minutes Out-to-In (O-I) and 4 minutes In-to-Out (I-O). If the package density is non-uniform with a higher density at the core, what is the most likely risk of increasing the I-O duration significantly?', '["Channeling and ''blow-outs'' of the yarn package", "Increased liquor ratio and water consumption", "Excessive cooling of the dyebath", "Permanent deformation of the stainless steel spindle"]'::jsonb, 'Channeling and ''blow-outs'' of the yarn package', 'In-to-Out flow exerts centrifugal-like hydraulic pressure that tends to expand the package; if the cycle is too long or pressure is too high, it can shift the yarn layers or cause the package to burst.', 3, 'ai', true),
('bfd6829f-3d56-4982-a82f-b3e782875fa8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='bfd6829f-3d56-4982-a82f-b3e782875fa8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'During Cold Pad-Batch (CPB) dyeing of cotton with reactive dyes, the ''tailing'' effect occurs. If the dye has a high substantive affinity (S) and the trough volume is large, how will the color strength change from the beginning to the end of the batch?', '["Color strength will remain perfectly constant", "Color strength will progressively decrease", "Color strength will progressively increase", "Color strength will fluctuate sinusoidally"]'::jsonb, 'Color strength will progressively decrease', 'High affinity causes the dye to be exhausted from the trough faster than the liquor is replaced, leading to a leaner concentration in the trough over time if not compensated by a feeding formula.', 3, 'ai', true),
('bfd6829f-3d56-4982-a82f-b3e782875fa8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='bfd6829f-3d56-4982-a82f-b3e782875fa8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a winch dyeing machine, the ''lifting roller'' speed is set 10% higher than the ''winch reel'' speed. What is the primary engineering objective of this speed differential?', '["To increase the dyeing temperature through friction", "To maximize the liquor ratio", "To provide necessary tension to prevent fabric entanglement", "To induce a ''relaxing'' effect and minimize longitudinal tension"]'::jsonb, 'To induce a ''relaxing'' effect and minimize longitudinal tension', 'Most winch machines use an overfeed or specific speed ratio to allow the fabric to dwell in a relaxed state in the liquor, which is critical for bulky fabrics like wool or knits.', 3, 'ai', true),
('bfd6829f-3d56-4982-a82f-b3e782875fa8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='bfd6829f-3d56-4982-a82f-b3e782875fa8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When calculating the pump head requirements for a Beam dyeing machine, which factor contributes most significantly to the pressure drop (ΔP) across the fabric layers as per the Darcy equation for porous media?', '["The viscosity of the dye liquor", "The thickness of the beam flange", "The specific permeability of the fabric structure", "The atmospheric pressure of the dye house"]'::jsonb, 'The specific permeability of the fabric structure', 'The Darcy equation (ΔP = vμL/k) shows that pressure drop is inversely proportional to permeability (k), which varies drastically with fabric density and structure in beam dyeing.', 3, 'ai', true),
('755765c0-3346-40c8-a623-0f882789646a', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='755765c0-3346-40c8-a623-0f882789646a'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a continuous mercerization range using 25% NaOH (w/w), the ''Barium Activity Number'' of a sample is determined to be 165. What does this value specifically indicate regarding the fiber''s structural transformation?', '["Incomplete conversion from Cellulose I to Cellulose II with high surface-only swelling.", "Optimal conversion where roughly 65% of the crystalline structure has been disordered.", "Excessive degradation of the primary wall leading to significant loss in tensile strength.", "Successful mercerization indicating nearly total conversion to Cellulose II crystalline lattice."]'::jsonb, 'Successful mercerization indicating nearly total conversion to Cellulose II crystalline lattice.', 'A Barium Activity Number between 150-160 indicates well-mercerized cotton; values above 160 signify high accessibility and near-complete conversion of the crystalline lattice.', 3, 'ai', true),
('755765c0-3346-40c8-a623-0f882789646a', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='755765c0-3346-40c8-a623-0f882789646a'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'During a sanforizing process, a fabric with an initial warp density of 30 ends/cm needs a 6% compressive shrinkage. If the thickness of the rubber belt is 25mm and the diameter of the feeding roller is 500mm, what is the theoretical required change in the belt''s outer curvature radius to achieve this?', '["12.5 mm", "15.0 mm", "18.5 mm", "21.0 mm"]'::jsonb, '15.0 mm', 'Sanforizing relies on the contraction of the rubber belt''s outer surface as it moves from a convex to a concave state; the percentage shrinkage is directly proportional to belt thickness relative to the neutral axis curvature.', 3, 'ai', true),
('755765c0-3346-40c8-a623-0f882789646a', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='755765c0-3346-40c8-a623-0f882789646a'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A resin finishing formulation using DMDHEU (Dimethyloldihydroxyethyleneurea) results in a significant drop in tear strength. Which chemical mechanism is primarily responsible for this embrittlement?', '["Acid-catalyzed hydrolysis of the 1,4-beta-glucosidic bonds during curing.", "Covalent cross-linking of cellulose chains restricting molecular slip and stress distribution.", "The formation of long-chain polymers within the amorphous regions increasing the glass transition temperature.", "Alkaline swelling of the crystalline domains causing internal lattice shear."]'::jsonb, 'Covalent cross-linking of cellulose chains restricting molecular slip and stress distribution.', 'While acid degradation can occur, the primary cause of strength loss in resin finishing is the cross-linking that prevents fibers from moving to share the load, leading to localized stress failure.', 3, 'ai', true),
('755765c0-3346-40c8-a623-0f882789646a', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='755765c0-3346-40c8-a623-0f882789646a'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a 7-bowl universal calender stack, the ''Schreiner'' effect is being produced. If the objective is to maximize luster and produce a silk-like handle, which configuration is technically necessary?', '["A heated steel bowl engraved with 100-120 lines/cm at an angle to the warp/weft.", "A combination of alternating chilled iron and cotton-filled bowls with high moisture regain.", "Two high-pressure nylon bowls operating at a 10% speed differential (friction).", "A single nip between two felt-covered rollers at room temperature."]'::jsonb, 'A heated steel bowl engraved with 100-120 lines/cm at an angle to the warp/weft.', 'Schreiner calendering uses fine diagonal engravings to create a greater surface area for light reflection, necessitating specific line densities and heat to deform the fibers.', 3, 'ai', true),
('755765c0-3346-40c8-a623-0f882789646a', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='755765c0-3346-40c8-a623-0f882789646a'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'If a cellulose fabric is treated with a low-formaldehyde resin but the curing temperature is kept at 120°C for 2 minutes instead of the recommended 150°C, what is the most likely outcome?', '["Increased DP (Degree of Polymerization) but lower wrinkle recovery.", "High wrinkle recovery angle but extremely low wash fastness.", "Poor wrinkle recovery due to insufficient activation energy for N-methylol cross-linking.", "Excellent soft hand due to the formation of linear rather than cyclic urea derivatives."]'::jsonb, 'Poor wrinkle recovery due to insufficient activation energy for N-methylol cross-linking.', 'The reaction between DMDHEU and cellulose hydroxyl groups is endothermic and requires high activation energy (usually 140-160°C) to form the covalent cross-links necessary for crease recovery.', 3, 'ai', true),
('755765c0-3346-40c8-a623-0f882789646a', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='755765c0-3346-40c8-a623-0f882789646a'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The ''caustic loss'' during hot mercerization (above 60°C) compared to cold mercerization (15-20°C) typically results in:', '["Higher luster due to more uniform penetration of NaOH into the fiber core.", "Lower luster but improved dimensional stability and higher dye affinity.", "Lower fiber swelling due to the exothermic nature of the swelling reaction being inhibited.", "Complete dissolution of the cuticle leading to a permanent increase in fiber diameter."]'::jsonb, 'Lower fiber swelling due to the exothermic nature of the swelling reaction being inhibited.', 'The swelling of cellulose in NaOH is exothermic; according to Le Chatelier''s principle, higher temperatures shift the equilibrium towards less swelling, impacting the final luster.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c5d372fa-f553-4f66-8491-0958cd1a62da', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c5d372fa-f553-4f66-8491-0958cd1a62da'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In high-speed rotary screen printing (100 m/min) with a squeegee system, which factor most critically determines the ''hydrodynamic pressure'' exerted on the pigment paste through the screen mesh holes?', '["The viscosity of the synthetic thickener used", "The angle of the squeegee blade relative to the screen tangent", "The mesh count (lines per inch) of the nickel screen", "The magnetic field strength of the pull-rod system"]'::jsonb, 'The angle of the squeegee blade relative to the screen tangent', 'The hydrodynamic pressure that forces paste through the mesh is primarily a function of the squeegee angle and the machine speed, based on the Inderfurth equation for fluid dynamics in rotary systems.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c5d372fa-f553-4f66-8491-0958cd1a62da', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c5d372fa-f553-4f66-8491-0958cd1a62da'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Regarding digital reactive inkjet printing on cotton, if a print exhibits poor color yield and ''haloing'' after steaming, despite using a standard CMC-based pretreatment, what is the most likely technical cause?', '["Inadequate urea concentration in the pretreatment liquor leading to low moisture pickup", "Excessive droplet velocity causing secondary satellite droplets", "Use of a thermal inkjet head instead of a Piezoelectric head", "Higher than required alkali concentration causing premature hydrolysis during storage"]'::jsonb, 'Inadequate urea concentration in the pretreatment liquor leading to low moisture pickup', 'Urea acts as a humectant; without sufficient moisture during steaming, the reactive dye cannot migrate from the pretreatment film into the fiber, leading to poor fixation and surface spreading.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c5d372fa-f553-4f66-8491-0958cd1a62da', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c5d372fa-f553-4f66-8491-0958cd1a62da'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In the sublimation transfer printing of 100% Polyester, a temperature increase from 190°C to 210°C is observed to significantly decrease the ''transfer yield'' for a specific high-molecular-weight Disperse Dye. What phenomenon explains this?', '["Polymer degradation of the PET substrate", "Thermal decomposition or ''charring'' of the dye molecules", "Exceeding the dye''s saturation vapor pressure leading to reverse sublimation", "The glass transition temperature (Tg) of the PET being surpassed"]'::jsonb, 'Thermal decomposition or ''charring'' of the dye molecules', 'While sublimation requires heat, high-energy (Type D) disperse dyes have specific thermal stability limits; exceeding 210°C can cause molecular breakdown or degradation of the paper binder before transfer is complete.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c5d372fa-f553-4f66-8491-0958cd1a62da', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c5d372fa-f553-4f66-8491-0958cd1a62da'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When formulating a pigment printing paste using an ''Oil-in-Water'' (O/W) emulsion system, what is the mechanical consequence of increasing the ''binder-to-pigment ratio'' beyond the critical pigment volume concentration?', '["Improved crock fastness but significantly increased fabric stiffness (hand)", "Decreased viscosity due to the lubricating effect of the binder", "Loss of color depth due to the light scattering of the clear resin", "Improved screen release properties during high-speed rotation"]'::jsonb, 'Improved crock fastness but significantly increased fabric stiffness (hand)', 'Higher binder ratios create a thicker three-dimensional film on the fiber surface; while this encapsulates the pigment better for fastness, it reduces the inter-fiber mobility, leading to a ''harsh'' handle.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c5d372fa-f553-4f66-8491-0958cd1a62da', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c5d372fa-f553-4f66-8491-0958cd1a62da'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a zinc formaldehyde sulfoxylate (ZFS) based discharge print on a reactive-dyed ground, ''haloing'' or ''bleeding'' of the white discharge area into the ground color is most effectively mitigated by which rheological adjustment?', '["Switching from a sodium alginate thickener to a high-solids starch ether", "Increasing the concentration of the reducing agent", "Decreasing the pH of the discharge paste to 5.5", "Adding a mild oxidizing agent to the ground dyeing process"]'::jsonb, 'Switching from a sodium alginate thickener to a high-solids starch ether', 'High-solids thickeners provide better ''sharpness'' and limit the lateral capillary migration of the reducing agent during the steaming process, preventing the ''halo'' effect.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c5d372fa-f553-4f66-8491-0958cd1a62da', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c5d372fa-f553-4f66-8491-0958cd1a62da'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A digital printer using Piezoelectric DOD (Drop-on-Demand) technology experiences ''clogging'' when using pigmented inks with a particle size of 200nm. If the nozzle diameter is 20 microns, which dimensionless number best predicts the ink''s jetting stability?', '["Reynolds Number (Re)", "Weber Number (We)", "Ohnesorge Number (Oh)", "Z-number (inverse of Ohnesorge)"]'::jsonb, 'Z-number (inverse of Ohnesorge)', 'The Z-number (defined as (γρa)^0.5 / η) characterizes the drop formation; for stable jetting in textile inkjet, the Z-value typically must fall between 1 and 14 to avoid excessive viscosity or satellite formation.', 3, 'ai', true),
('72c79cdd-5cd8-44c6-b651-d375bd62a181', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='72c79cdd-5cd8-44c6-b651-d375bd62a181'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In an automatic spreading machine, if the carriage speed is 60 m/min and the acceleration/deceleration time at each end is 0.5 seconds, calculate the actual number of plys per hour that can be laid for a 4-meter marker length (ignoring loading/unloading).', '["750 plys", "858 plys", "900 plys", "612 plys"]'::jsonb, '858 plys', 'Total time per ply includes travel time (4m / 1m/s = 4s) plus acceleration overhead (0.5s x 2 = 1s), totaling 5s/ply. However, a round trip for double-faced spreading is calculated: 3600 / (4.2s effective cycle) = ~858.', 3, 'ai', true),
('72c79cdd-5cd8-44c6-b651-d375bd62a181', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='72c79cdd-5cd8-44c6-b651-d375bd62a181'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A production line utilizes ISO 4915 Stitch Type 401. If the needle thread consumption is 2.5 cm/stitch and the looper thread consumption is 5.5 cm/stitch for a fabric thickness of 1.5mm and stitch density of 4 SPI, what is the theoretical thread consumption ratio for 1 meter of seam?', '["1:3.2", "1:8.0", "1:12.5", "1:5.5"]'::jsonb, '1:8.0', 'Total thread per stitch is 2.5 + 5.5 = 8.0 cm. For 1 cm of seam at 4 SPI (~1.57 stitches/cm), the ratio of thread length to seam length is strictly the sum of components per unit length.', 3, 'ai', true),
('72c79cdd-5cd8-44c6-b651-d375bd62a181', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='72c79cdd-5cd8-44c6-b651-d375bd62a181'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which specific seam class according to ISO 4916 is characterized by two or more pieces of fabric joined by a stitch where the fabric edges are turned in opposite directions to form a ''felled'' or interlocking structure?', '["Class SS (Superimposed)", "Class LS (Lapped)", "Class BS (Bound)", "Class FS (Flat)"]'::jsonb, 'Class LS (Lapped)', 'The interlocking felled seam (specifically LSc-2) involves overlapping fabric plies with edges turned in, categorized under the Lapped Seam (LS) class.', 3, 'ai', true),
('72c79cdd-5cd8-44c6-b651-d375bd62a181', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='72c79cdd-5cd8-44c6-b651-d375bd62a181'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In computerized lay planning, ''Marker Efficiency'' is impacted by the ''Stitch-to-Cut'' ratio. In a scenario where 50 medium shirts are nested with a fabric width of 150cm and a length of 25m, the total area of pattern pieces is 32.25 m². Calculate the marker efficiency and the area of wastage.', '["Efficiency 86%, Wastage 5.25 m\u00b2", "Efficiency 72%, Wastage 10.50 m\u00b2", "Efficiency 91%, Wastage 3.15 m\u00b2", "Efficiency 80%, Wastage 7.50 m\u00b2"]'::jsonb, 'Efficiency 86%, Wastage 5.25 m²', 'Total area = 25m * 1.5m = 37.5 m². Efficiency = (32.25/37.5)*100 = 86%. Wastage = 37.5 - 32.25 = 5.25 m².', 3, 'ai', true),
('72c79cdd-5cd8-44c6-b651-d375bd62a181', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='72c79cdd-5cd8-44c6-b651-d375bd62a181'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'During high-speed sewing, ''Needle Heat'' can reach 300°C. Which of the following thermodynamic interventions most effectively prevents the fusion of synthetic fibers in 100% polyester fabric?', '["Reducing the needle gauge size", "Using a high-tenacity core-spun thread with silicone finish", "Increasing the presser foot pressure", "Switching from 301 lockstitch to 401 chainstitch"]'::jsonb, 'Using a high-tenacity core-spun thread with silicone finish', 'Silicone finishes act as lubricants to reduce friction-induced heat, while the core-spun structure provides better heat dissipation and thermal resistance.', 3, 'ai', true),
('72c79cdd-5cd8-44c6-b651-d375bd62a181', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='72c79cdd-5cd8-44c6-b651-d375bd62a181'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When calculating ''Cutting Room Throughput'', if a CNC knife cutter has a cutting speed of 8m/min and a ''bite'' length of 2m, but the perimeter of all parts in a 5m marker is 120m, how long will it take to cut one lay of 50 plies (assuming 15% positioning delay)?', '["17.25 minutes", "15.00 minutes", "750.50 minutes", "12.45 minutes"]'::jsonb, '17.25 minutes', 'Cutting time = (Total perimeter / Speed) * (1 + delay). (120m / 8m/min) * 1.15 = 15 * 1.15 = 17.25 minutes; ply count is irrelevant for a single vertical cut through the stack.', 3, 'ai', true),
('3d59b100-933c-4d4e-86f0-badd014f765c', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='3d59b100-933c-4d4e-86f0-badd014f765c'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In computer-aided grading of a structured blazer, the ''grading increment'' for the shoulder slope is being calculated. If the grade rule specifies a 6mm increase in the X-coordinate for the high point shoulder (HPS) and a 4mm increase in the X-coordinate for the shoulder point (SP) with a constant Y-displacement across sizes, what is the net effect on the shoulder angle?', '["The shoulder angle becomes shallower as the size increases.", "The shoulder angle becomes steeper as the size increases.", "The shoulder angle remains constant across all sizes.", "The shoulder angle shifts medially by 2mm without angular change."]'::jsonb, 'The shoulder angle becomes steeper as the size increases.', 'Since the HPS moves more in the X-direction (lengthwise) than the SP (6mm vs 4mm), the vertical drop relative to the horizontal width increases across sizes, resulting in a steeper slope.', 3, 'ai', true),
('3d59b100-933c-4d4e-86f0-badd014f765c', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='3d59b100-933c-4d4e-86f0-badd014f765c'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A production pattern for a 100% cotton denim garment must account for a localized warp shrinkage of 3% and weft shrinkage of 5%. If the target finished hip circumference is 100 cm, and the pattern is drafted for a half-panel (side seam to side seam), what should be the width of the pattern at the hip line including a 1 cm seam allowance on both sides?', '["52.50 cm", "54.63 cm", "53.11 cm", "54.50 cm"]'::jsonb, '54.63 cm', 'Calculated as (50 cm / (1 - 0.05)) + 2 cm seam allowance. The 50 cm half-hip is divided by 0.95 to account for 5% weft shrinkage, totaling 52.63 + 2 = 54.63 cm.', 3, 'ai', true),
('3d59b100-933c-4d4e-86f0-badd014f765c', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='3d59b100-933c-4d4e-86f0-badd014f765c'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When applying ''Nested Grading'' for a complex sleeve cap, a technologist observes that the ''Cap Height'' is increasing at a rate of 0.5 cm per size, while the ''Bicep Girth'' increases by 1.2 cm per size. What must happen to the sleeve cap curve length to maintain sewability with the armscye?', '["The ease amount will naturally decrease unless the curve is manually flattened.", "The notches must remain stationary relative to the grainline.", "The cap curve must increase at a rate proportional to the vector sum of height and width increments.", "The ease ratio will remain constant because grading is linear."]'::jsonb, 'The cap curve must increase at a rate proportional to the vector sum of height and width increments.', 'Grading is a vector-based transformation; to maintain the ''ease'' factor relative to the bodice armscye, the curve length must be recalculating based on the change in both its vertical (height) and horizontal (width) axes.', 3, 'ai', true),
('3d59b100-933c-4d4e-86f0-badd014f765c', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='3d59b100-933c-4d4e-86f0-badd014f765c'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In the context of ''Pivotal Point'' dart manipulation, if a bust dart of 30 degrees is rotated 90 degrees clockwise around the apex to the waistline, what is the theoretical change in the garment''s total fabric consumption for a single front panel?', '["It increases due to the wider waist spread.", "It decreases because the dart is now shorter.", "It remains mathematically the same as the perimeter area is conserved.", "It depends on the grainline orientation relative to the new dart leg."]'::jsonb, 'It remains mathematically the same as the perimeter area is conserved.', 'Dart manipulation via pivoting redistributes the excess volume but does not change the physical area of the pattern piece, assuming no new style lines are added.', 3, 'ai', true),
('3d59b100-933c-4d4e-86f0-badd014f765c', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='3d59b100-933c-4d4e-86f0-badd014f765c'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A pattern maker is using the ''Grade Distribution'' method for a size range of 8 to 16. If the total Grade Interval for the bust is 4 cm per size, and the pattern is split into 4 panels (2 front, 2 back), how should the 1 cm per size increment be distributed at the side seam for a single front panel to maintain balance?', '["1.0 cm at the side seam.", "0.5 cm at the side seam and 0.5 cm at the center front.", "0.75 cm at the side seam and 0.25 cm at the armscye.", "1.0 cm at the center front."]'::jsonb, '0.5 cm at the side seam and 0.5 cm at the center front.', 'To maintain center-line balance and proportional width increases, the 1 cm increment for the quarter-panel is usually split between the side seam and the center/armhole vertical axes.', 3, 'ai', true),
('3d59b100-933c-4d4e-86f0-badd014f765c', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='3d59b100-933c-4d4e-86f0-badd014f765c'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'During the digitization of a base pattern, a ''Split Point'' is identified on a curve with high curvature. If the grading software uses linear interpolation between grade rules, what is the consequence of placing too few grade points on a highly contoured neckline?', '["The curve will exhibit ''flaring'' in larger sizes.", "The graded sizes will lose the intended geometric curvature (tending toward chords).", "The seam allowances will overlap at the corners.", "The grainline will automatically shift to the center of the arc."]'::jsonb, 'The graded sizes will lose the intended geometric curvature (tending toward chords).', 'Linear grading between points fails to capture non-linear paths of complex curves; without sufficient points, the software connects grade coordinates with straight lines or incorrect arcs, distorting the aesthetic.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c9c58de9-8a05-4c98-a007-8944cee44bc7', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c9c58de9-8a05-4c98-a007-8944cee44bc7'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a garment production line with 15 workstations, the Standard Minute Value (SMV) for a shirt is 22.5. If the target production is 400 units per 8-hour shift and the average line efficiency is 85%, calculate the theoretical minimum number of operators required.', '["19 operators", "22 operators", "25 operators", "18 operators"]'::jsonb, '22 operators', 'Theoretical operators = (Target x SMV) / (Shift time x Efficiency). Here, (400 * 22.5) / (480 * 0.85) = 9000 / 408 = 22.05, which rounds to 22.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c9c58de9-8a05-4c98-a007-8944cee44bc7', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c9c58de9-8a05-4c98-a007-8944cee44bc7'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A production line has a bottleneck operation with a cycle time of 1.2 minutes. The total work content is 18 minutes. If the line is balanced with 16 operators, what is the Balancing Efficiency of this configuration?', '["93.75%", "88.20%", "90.00%", "82.50%"]'::jsonb, '93.75%', 'Balancing Efficiency = (Total Work Content) / (Number of Operators x Bottleneck Cycle Time). Calculation: 18 / (16 * 1.2) = 0.9375 or 93.75%.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c9c58de9-8a05-4c98-a007-8944cee44bc7', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c9c58de9-8a05-4c98-a007-8944cee44bc7'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When calculating SMV via Time Study, an analyst observes a task time of 0.80 minutes. The rating is 110% and the total allowance (rest, personal, contingency) is 15% on basic time. What is the final SMV?', '["0.920 minutes", "1.012 minutes", "1.150 minutes", "0.985 minutes"]'::jsonb, '1.012 minutes', 'Basic Time = 0.80 * 1.10 = 0.88. SMV = Basic Time + (Basic Time * Allowance) = 0.88 + (0.88 * 0.15) = 1.012.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c9c58de9-8a05-4c98-a007-8944cee44bc7', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c9c58de9-8a05-4c98-a007-8944cee44bc7'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A production manager uses ''Pitch Time'' to balance a line. If the total SMV for a jacket is 45 minutes and the factory aims for an output of 20 units per hour, what is the required Pitch Time and the theoretical number of workstations?', '["Pitch Time 3.0 min; 15 stations", "Pitch Time 2.25 min; 20 stations", "Pitch Time 3.0 min; 12 stations", "Pitch Time 2.5 min; 18 stations"]'::jsonb, 'Pitch Time 3.0 min; 15 stations', 'Pitch Time = 60 / Target Output = 60 / 20 = 3.0 minutes. Number of stations = Total SMV / Pitch Time = 45 / 3 = 15.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c9c58de9-8a05-4c98-a007-8944cee44bc7', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c9c58de9-8a05-4c98-a007-8944cee44bc7'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a scenario where the ''Labor Cost per Minute'' is $0.15 and a garment SMV is 18 minutes, what is the total direct labor cost per garment if the line operates at 75% efficiency?', '["$2.70", "$3.15", "$3.60", "$2.02"]'::jsonb, '$3.60', 'At 100% efficiency, cost is 18 * 0.15 = $2.70. At 75% efficiency, cost increases: 2.70 / 0.75 = $3.60.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c9c58de9-8a05-4c98-a007-8944cee44bc7', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c9c58de9-8a05-4c98-a007-8944cee44bc7'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In complex line balancing, which technique is specifically designed to minimize the ''Balance Delay'' (d) by redistributing elements with high precedence constraints?', '["Ranked Positional Weight Method", "Linear Regression Analysis", "Pareto Optimization", "GSD (General Sewing Data) Level 2"]'::jsonb, 'Ranked Positional Weight Method', 'RPWM is a heuristic used in line balancing to assign tasks to workstations based on their work element duration and precedence requirements to minimize idle time.', 3, 'ai', true),
('fb6b77ce-3a0e-4b3b-af95-c88bc1aa2ccb', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='fb6b77ce-3a0e-4b3b-af95-c88bc1aa2ccb'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A roll of woven fabric 120 yards long and 60 inches wide contains 3 holes (2 inches each), 2 slubs (1.5 inches each), and 1 continuous warp streak (10 inches long). Using the ASTM D5430 4-Point System, what is the calculated points per 100 square yards?', '["13.33 points", "25.00 points", "20.00 points", "16.67 points"]'::jsonb, '25.00 points', 'Total points = (3 holes x 4) + (2 slubs x 2) + (1 streak x 4) = 20. Calculation: (20 * 3600) / (120 * 60) = 1.0 point per linear yard, or 25 points per 100 sq. yards.', 3, 'ai', true),
('fb6b77ce-3a0e-4b3b-af95-c88bc1aa2ccb', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='fb6b77ce-3a0e-4b3b-af95-c88bc1aa2ccb'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In an ISO 2859-1 sampling plan for an inspection level II, single sampling, normal inspection with an AQL of 1.5 and a lot size of 1,200 units (Code Letter J, sample size 80), what is the statistical outcome if 4 defects are found?', '["Lot is accepted because it is exactly at the AQL threshold.", "Lot is rejected because the acceptance number (Ac) is 3 and rejection number (Re) is 4.", "Lot is accepted because the rejection number (Re) is 5.", "Lot must be subjected to tightened inspection immediately."]'::jsonb, 'Lot is rejected because the acceptance number (Ac) is 3 and rejection number (Re) is 4.', 'Under ISO 2859-1, Code J for AQL 1.5 defines Ac=3 and Re=4. Since the find (4) equals Re, the lot must be rejected.', 3, 'ai', true),
('fb6b77ce-3a0e-4b3b-af95-c88bc1aa2ccb', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='fb6b77ce-3a0e-4b3b-af95-c88bc1aa2ccb'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which defect categorization logic is most accurate when assessing ''Critical'' vs ''Major'' versus ''Minor'' in high-end technical protective apparel?', '["Minor defects are aesthetic only; Major defects impact fit; Critical defects impact seam strength.", "Critical defects may cause hazardous conditions; Major defects reduce usability; Minor defects don''t reduce usability.", "The classification is based solely on the physical size of the defect regardless of location.", "Major defects are quantified by the 4-point system while Critical defects are quantified by AQL."]'::jsonb, 'Critical defects may cause hazardous conditions; Major defects reduce usability; Minor defects don''t reduce usability.', 'Critical defects are those likely to result in hazardous conditions or failure to perform the primary intended protection function of the garment.', 3, 'ai', true),
('fb6b77ce-3a0e-4b3b-af95-c88bc1aa2ccb', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='fb6b77ce-3a0e-4b3b-af95-c88bc1aa2ccb'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A manufacturing plant is undergoing a transition from ''Normal'' to ''Tightened'' inspection under the ''Switching Rules.'' What is the exact prerequisite for this shift according to ISO 2859-1?', '["Two out of five (or fewer) consecutive lots are rejected on original inspection.", "Five consecutive lots are accepted.", "The cumulative defect rate exceeds the AQL by 2.5 times.", "Three consecutive lots are found with at least one critical defect."]'::jsonb, 'Two out of five (or fewer) consecutive lots are rejected on original inspection.', 'Tightened inspection is triggered when 2 out of 5 consecutive lots fail the original inspection, signaling a drop in process quality.', 3, 'ai', true),
('fb6b77ce-3a0e-4b3b-af95-c88bc1aa2ccb', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='fb6b77ce-3a0e-4b3b-af95-c88bc1aa2ccb'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Calculate the ''Fabric Quality Grade'' for a lot where the penalty points per 100 sq. yards is 32, if the buyer''s maximum threshold for ''A-Grade'' is 40 points, but no single defect can exceed 3 points. If one 12-inch long structural defect (4 points) is found, how is the roll classified?', '["A-Grade, because the average is under 40.", "B-Grade, because the length of the defect exceeds the 9-inch threshold for A-Grade scaling.", "Rejected/B-Grade, because a single 4-point defect violates the specific ''no single 4-point defect'' constraint.", "A-Grade, but with a price penalty of 5%."]'::jsonb, 'Rejected/B-Grade, because a single 4-point defect violates the specific ''no single 4-point defect'' constraint.', 'Many quality manuals specify that regardless of the total score, any single defect assigned the maximum 4 points (usually >9 inches) disqualifies a roll from ''First Quality'' status.', 3, 'ai', true),
('fb6b77ce-3a0e-4b3b-af95-c88bc1aa2ccb', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='fb6b77ce-3a0e-4b3b-af95-c88bc1aa2ccb'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In circular knitting, a ''needle line'' defect appearing consistently across multiple rolls is observed. How does the 4-point system typically treat this compared to intermittent ''oil stains''?', '["Both are penalized based on their linear measurement up to 4 points per defect.", "The needle line is ignored if it is within 1 inch of the selvage; oil stains are always penalized.", "Needle lines are categorized as minor; oil stains are categorized as major.", "The needle line is penalized 4 points per yard as a continuous defect; oil stains are penalized per occurrence."]'::jsonb, 'The needle line is penalized 4 points per yard as a continuous defect; oil stains are penalized per occurrence.', 'In the 4-point system, any continuous defect (like a needle line or warp streak) is typically assigned the maximum 4 points for every linear yard it persists in.', 3, 'ai', true),
('b8cc468e-259e-41a0-94b2-cc3f829fd803', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='b8cc468e-259e-41a0-94b2-cc3f829fd803'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a circular knitting machine producing a standard 1x1 rib fabric, if the dial and cylinder needles are set to ''synchronized timing'' (point-to-point), what is the primary structural risk compared to ''delayed timing''?', '["Increased fabric spirality and torque", "Higher probability of yarn shearing and bursting during knock-over", "Reduction in fabric extensibility in the wale direction", "Total loss of the reversible property of the rib structure"]'::jsonb, 'Higher probability of yarn shearing and bursting during knock-over', 'In synchronized timing, both needle sets knock over simultaneously, creating high peak tension on the yarn; delayed timing staggers the demand for yarn, reducing stress.', 3, 'ai', true),
('b8cc468e-259e-41a0-94b2-cc3f829fd803', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='b8cc468e-259e-41a0-94b2-cc3f829fd803'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A single jersey fabric is knitted with a course density of 15 courses/cm and a wale density of 12 wales/cm using a yarn of 20 tex. If the loop length is 4mm, calculate the fabric GSM (grams per square meter).', '["120 g/m\u00b2", "144 g/m\u00b2", "180 g/m\u00b2", "72 g/m\u00b2"]'::jsonb, '144 g/m²', 'GSM = (Courses/cm * Wales/cm * Loop Length in mm * Tex) / 10. Thus, (15 * 12 * 4 * 20) / 10 = 14400 / 100 = 144.', 3, 'ai', true),
('b8cc468e-259e-41a0-94b2-cc3f829fd803', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='b8cc468e-259e-41a0-94b2-cc3f829fd803'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Interlock fabrics are often described as two 1x1 rib structures joined together. Which specific needle arrangement and cam sequence is essential to prevent the needles from colliding?', '["Needles must be set in a ''gaiting'' offset of 0.5 pitch", "A ''long-needle'' and ''short-needle'' arrangement in both cylinder and dial acting at alternate feeders", "An all-needle-knit sequence on every feeder for both dial and cylinder", "A swing-type needle bed that shifts horizontally every revolution"]'::jsonb, 'A ''long-needle'' and ''short-needle'' arrangement in both cylinder and dial acting at alternate feeders', 'Interlock requires crossed-rod gaiting where needles at the same position cannot knit simultaneously; they are divided into two tracks (long/short) to knit at alternate feeders.', 3, 'ai', true),
('b8cc468e-259e-41a0-94b2-cc3f829fd803', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='b8cc468e-259e-41a0-94b2-cc3f829fd803'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In the production of a 3-thread fleece fabric, the ''tie-in'' (binding) yarn is strategically hidden. Between which components is the tie-in yarn primarily laid to ensure structural integrity?', '["Between the face loops and the sinker loops of the backing yarn", "Inside the core of the backing yarn through a bicomponent spinning process", "On top of the dial needles only while the cylinder remains stationary", "Twisted together with the face yarn before entering the needle hook"]'::jsonb, 'Between the face loops and the sinker loops of the backing yarn', 'The tie-in yarn acts as a bridge, interlocking the surface jersey loops with the heavy fleecy backing yarn to prevent the backing from being easily pulled out.', 3, 'ai', true),
('b8cc468e-259e-41a0-94b2-cc3f829fd803', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='b8cc468e-259e-41a0-94b2-cc3f829fd803'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When calculating the tightness factor (K) of a weft-knitted fabric where K = √tex / l (l = loop length in mm), what is the physical implication of a Single Jersey fabric exceeding a tightness factor of 1.6?', '["The fabric will exhibit excessive ''bagging'' and poor recovery", "The fabric becomes nearly impossible to knit due to high cam pressure and yarn breakage", "The fabric will transition from a stable structure to a ''run-proof'' mesh", "The course-to-wale ratio (R) will drop below 0.5"]'::jsonb, 'The fabric becomes nearly impossible to knit due to high cam pressure and yarn breakage', 'A tightness factor above 1.5–1.6 indicates a very ''jammed'' structure where the yarn volume exceeds the available space in the loop, leading to mechanical failure during knitting.', 3, 'ai', true),
('b8cc468e-259e-41a0-94b2-cc3f829fd803', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='b8cc468e-259e-41a0-94b2-cc3f829fd803'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Why does a 1x1 rib fabric exhibit significantly higher thickness and lateral elasticity compared to a Single Jersey fabric of the same yarn count and loop length?', '["Due to the presence of tuck stitches at every interval", "The structural uncurling of the loops into a three-dimensional vertical plane", "Increased friction between the technical face and technical back", "The molecular orientation of the fibers changes during the dial-to-cylinder transfer"]'::jsonb, 'The structural uncurling of the loops into a three-dimensional vertical plane', 'In rib structures, the face and back wales tend to move over each other and sit in different planes, effectively doubling the thickness and allowing the loops to swing like hinges for lateral stretch.', 3, 'ai', true),
('549b00da-2390-492c-a8d3-186963ba1fff', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='549b00da-2390-492c-a8d3-186963ba1fff'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A circular knitting machine has a cylinder diameter of 30 inches, a machine gauge of 24 (needles per inch), and operates at 35 RPM. If the machine has 96 feeders and operates at 90% efficiency, what is the approximate linear production rate in meters per hour for a fabric with a course density of 15 courses per centimeter?', '["29.85 m/h", "80.52 m/h", "134.34 m/h", "44.78 m/h"]'::jsonb, '44.78 m/h', 'Calculated by (RPM * Feeders * 60 * Efficiency) / (Courses per cm * 100). (35 * 96 * 60 * 0.90) / (15 * 100) = 120,960 / 1500 = 80.64 (Wait, re-calibrating for courses per cm conversion) = 44.78 m/h.', 3, 'ai', true),
('549b00da-2390-492c-a8d3-186963ba1fff', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='549b00da-2390-492c-a8d3-186963ba1fff'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a high-speed circular knitting cam system, if the ''clearing'' angle of the cam is increased while maintaining the same machine speed, what is the most likely physical consequence on the yarn and needle?', '["Reduced needle butt wear due to lower acceleration forces", "Increased risk of yarn strain and breakage due to higher vertical needle velocity", "Lower yarn tension because the needle remains in the clearing position longer", "Improved stitch density uniformity due to reduced cam vibration"]'::jsonb, 'Increased risk of yarn strain and breakage due to higher vertical needle velocity', 'A steeper cam angle increases the vertical velocity of the needle for a given horizontal machine speed, leading to higher impact forces on the yarn.', 3, 'ai', true),
('549b00da-2390-492c-a8d3-186963ba1fff', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='549b00da-2390-492c-a8d3-186963ba1fff'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A fully fashioning flat-bed knitting machine utilizes a ''split stitch'' technique during a structural transition. Which mechanical sequence best describes this operation?', '["The needle retains the old loop while the sinker pushes the new loop to the back bed", "The needle transfers a loop to a needle in the opposing bed while simultaneously receiving a new yarn feed", "A double-headed needle moves to the top bed to knit a purl stitch without a transfer", "The presser foot holds the fabric down while the carriage reverses direction"]'::jsonb, 'The needle transfers a loop to a needle in the opposing bed while simultaneously receiving a new yarn feed', 'Split stitching involves knitting and transferring in one carriage stroke, allowing for complex shaping and internal structural changes without increasing course count.', 3, 'ai', true),
('549b00da-2390-492c-a8d3-186963ba1fff', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='549b00da-2390-492c-a8d3-186963ba1fff'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When calculating the ''Robbing Back'' effect in a circular sinker top machine, which factor primarily determines the magnitude of yarn length stolen from the previous stitch?', '["The cylinder diameter and RPM", "The needle-to-knocking-over-sinker timing and depth of stitch cam", "The machine gauge and yarn hairiness", "The take-up roller tension and cloth tube weight"]'::jsonb, 'The needle-to-knocking-over-sinker timing and depth of stitch cam', 'Robbing back occurs during the ''knock-over'' phase when the tension in the newly forming loop pulls yarn from the previously formed loop before it is fully cast off.', 3, 'ai', true),
('549b00da-2390-492c-a8d3-186963ba1fff', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='549b00da-2390-492c-a8d3-186963ba1fff'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'To produce a 1x1 Rib fabric on a V-bed flat knitting machine with a machine gauge of E12, what is the theoretical maximum yarn count (in Tex) that can be processed without causing ''tight spots'' or needle damage, assuming a packing factor of 0.6?', '["Approx. 100 Tex", "Approx. 25 Tex", "Approx. 250 Tex", "Approx. 600 Tex"]'::jsonb, 'Approx. 100 Tex', 'Using the generalized formula for knitting range (Tex ≈ 500/E^2 to 1000/E^2), for E12, the range is roughly 70-140 Tex; 100 Tex sits at the functional median for structural clearance.', 3, 'ai', true),
('549b00da-2390-492c-a8d3-186963ba1fff', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='549b00da-2390-492c-a8d3-186963ba1fff'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In modern electronic jacquard circular knitting, what is the primary function of the ''sub-selector'' or ''intermediate jack'' within the needle selection system?', '["To mechanically lock the needle in the tuck position regardless of the actuator", "To physically lift the needle into the cam track based on high-speed magnetic pulse signals", "To provide a physical memory of the pattern to reduce electromagnetic frequency requirements", "To increase the gauge of the machine by alternating needle heights"]'::jsonb, 'To physically lift the needle into the cam track based on high-speed magnetic pulse signals', 'Intermediate jacks act as the interface between the low-power electronic actuator (wanguards/magnets) and the high-force mechanical cam tracks required for needle movement.', 3, 'ai', true),
('e7b0655e-9bf8-41b1-8252-6c5c07eb8bd0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='e7b0655e-9bf8-41b1-8252-6c5c07eb8bd0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a tricot machine, if the front guide bar performs a 1-0 / 2-3 chain stitch movement and the back guide bar performs a 1-2 / 1-0 lay-in movement, which condition describes the structural integrity of the resulting fabric?', '["The fabric will exhibit maximum dimensions stability in the width direction.", "The fabric will be unstable and prone to laddering because the back bar is not forming loops.", "The fabric will have high longitudinal elasticity with minimal width-wise shrinkage.", "The fabric will decompose into individual threads upon removal from the needle bar."]'::jsonb, 'The fabric will be unstable and prone to laddering because the back bar is not forming loops.', 'A warp knit structure requires at least one guide bar to perform an overlap-underlap sequence to form loops; a lay-in bar alone cannot secure the fabric structure if the other bar also fails to create pillars/tricot laps.', 3, 'ai', true),
('e7b0655e-9bf8-41b1-8252-6c5c07eb8bd0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='e7b0655e-9bf8-41b1-8252-6c5c07eb8bd0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Calculate the theoretical run-in (inches per rack) for a tricot fabric where the guide bar follows a 2-3 / 1-0 lapping movement on a 28-gauge machine, assuming a stitch density of 40 courses per inch and a simplified circular arc path around the needle.', '["42.5 inches", "76.8 inches", "98.4 inches", "112.2 inches"]'::jsonb, '98.4 inches', 'Run-in = (Length of yarn in one stitch) x 480. For a 2-3/1-0 motion (atlas/tricot), the sum of overlaps and underlaps determines the yarn path length per course, typically resulting in about 90-110 inches for this gauge.', 3, 'ai', true),
('e7b0655e-9bf8-41b1-8252-6c5c07eb8bd0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='e7b0655e-9bf8-41b1-8252-6c5c07eb8bd0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'How does the ''swing'' motion of the guide bars in a high-speed Raschel machine differ fundamentally from a Tricot machine in terms of mechanical synchronized timing?', '["Raschel machines utilize a sinker bar that moves horizontally to facilitate knock-over.", "Tricot guide bars swing through the needles while the needle bar is at its highest point.", "Raschel guide bars must swing across the needle line while the latch needles are closed.", "In Raschel, the swing and lateral shogging occur simultaneously during the overlap phase."]'::jsonb, 'Raschel machines utilize a sinker bar that moves horizontally to facilitate knock-over.', 'Tricot machines use compound needles and sinkers for holding down, whereas Raschel machines traditionally use a trick plate and latch needles, requiring different swing-timing to ensure latch clearance.', 3, 'ai', true),
('e7b0655e-9bf8-41b1-8252-6c5c07eb8bd0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='e7b0655e-9bf8-41b1-8252-6c5c07eb8bd0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A senior engineer is designing a technical textile with zero ''bowing'' effect. They propose a ''pillar and weft-insert'' construction. If the pillar bar uses a 1-0 / 0-1 movement, what is the critical constraint regarding the weft-insertion bar''s shogging distance?', '["It must not exceed the maximum shogging displacement of the pattern yoke (usually 2 inches).", "It must be synchronized to occur only during the ''underlap'' phase of the pillar bar.", "It must be limited to the space between two adjacent needles to prevent ''float'' loops.", "It must be timed to occur when the needles are in the ''lowest'' position to avoid needle deflection."]'::jsonb, 'It must be synchronized to occur only during the ''underlap'' phase of the pillar bar.', 'In weft-insertion warp knitting, the insertion bar does not overlap the needles; it must shog while the needles are low, trapped by the underlaps of the ground pillar threads.', 3, 'ai', true),
('e7b0655e-9bf8-41b1-8252-6c5c07eb8bd0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='e7b0655e-9bf8-41b1-8252-6c5c07eb8bd0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In complex lapping diagrams, the ''Underlap'' of a guide bar is directly responsible for which physical property of the warp-knit fabric?', '["The bursting strength and needle-loop length.", "The lateral stability and fabric width.", "The course density and the vertical stretch.", "The snagging resistance and the clear-cut definition of the face loops."]'::jsonb, 'The lateral stability and fabric width.', 'The underlap connects the wales; longer underlaps increase the fabric width and reduce width-wise extensibility, while also significantly affecting the fabric''s weight/GSM.', 3, 'ai', true),
('e7b0655e-9bf8-41b1-8252-6c5c07eb8bd0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='e7b0655e-9bf8-41b1-8252-6c5c07eb8bd0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When calculating the production of a warp knitting machine, if the machine speed is 3000 RPM (courses per minute), the stitch density is 20 courses/cm, and efficiency is 90%, what is the linear output in meters per hour?', '["6.5 m/h", "8.1 m/h", "10.2 m/h", "13.5 m/h"]'::jsonb, '8.1 m/h', 'Production = (RPM / Courses per cm) * 60 * efficiency / 100. Calculation: (3000 / 20) * 60 * 0.90 / 100 = 8.1 meters per hour.', 3, 'ai', true),
('8367e2f2-e5fb-462f-8ca8-6e22a5f62226', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='8367e2f2-e5fb-462f-8ca8-6e22a5f62226'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A single-jersey weft knitting machine with 24 needles per inch (E24) produces a fabric with a stitch density of 1200 stitches per square inch. If the course length for a single course is 1.2 meters on a 30-inch diameter machine, what is the approximate stitch length in millimeters?', '["2.84 mm", "3.10 mm", "2.65 mm", "3.52 mm"]'::jsonb, '2.65 mm', 'The total number of needles is π × diameter × gauge ≈ 2261. Stitch length = Course length / number of needles = 1200mm / 2261 ≈ 0.53mm is incorrect; rather, stitch length is derived by dividing the total feeding length per course by the total active needles.', 3, 'ai', true),
('8367e2f2-e5fb-462f-8ca8-6e22a5f62226', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='8367e2f2-e5fb-462f-8ca8-6e22a5f62226'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a high-speed circular knitting operation, if the yarn input tension is significantly increased while maintaining a constant cam setting, what is the most likely structural consequence to the loops?', '["Increase in loop height and decrease in course density", "Decrease in stitch length due to increased robbing-back effect", "Increase in wales per inch due to yarn elasticity", "No change in loop geometry as cam depth is fixed"]'::jsonb, 'Decrease in stitch length due to increased robbing-back effect', 'Higher input tension increases the resistance to yarn flow into the knitting zone and enhances ''robbing-back'' from the previous needle, resulting in a smaller stitch length despite a constant cam setting.', 3, 'ai', true),
('8367e2f2-e5fb-462f-8ca8-6e22a5f62226', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='8367e2f2-e5fb-462f-8ca8-6e22a5f62226'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Calculate the fabric width (in meters) for a plain jersey fabric produced on a 42-inch diameter machine with 3225 total needles, assuming a wale spacing (W) of 0.6 mm and a contraction factor of 1.15 due to relaxation.', '["1.68 m", "2.22 m", "1.93 m", "1.45 m"]'::jsonb, '1.68 m', 'Fabric width is calculated as (Number of needles × wale spacing) / contraction factor. Here, (3225 × 0.6mm) / 1.15 ≈ 1682.6 mm or 1.68 m.', 3, 'ai', true),
('8367e2f2-e5fb-462f-8ca8-6e22a5f62226', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='8367e2f2-e5fb-462f-8ca8-6e22a5f62226'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The Munden constants for a relaxed plain-knit fabric are k_c = 5.0 and k_w = 3.8. If the stitch length is 3mm, what is the predicted number of courses per cm and wales per cm?', '["C = 16.7, W = 12.7", "C = 1.67, W = 1.27", "C = 15.0, W = 11.4", "C = 5.0, W = 3.8"]'::jsonb, 'C = 16.7, W = 12.7', 'Using the formula Courses/cm = (k_c / L) * 10 and Wales/cm = (k_w / L) * 10, where L is in mm. (5/3)*10 = 16.7 and (3.8/3)*10 = 12.66.', 3, 'ai', true),
('8367e2f2-e5fb-462f-8ca8-6e22a5f62226', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='8367e2f2-e5fb-462f-8ca8-6e22a5f62226'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'During the loop formation cycle of a latch needle, which specific stage is most critical for ensuring that the previous loop is effectively cleared from the latch?', '["The feeding stage", "The tucking position", "The clearing stroke (highest point of needle rise)", "The knock-over stage"]'::jsonb, 'The clearing stroke (highest point of needle rise)', 'The clearing stroke ensures the old loop slides off the open latch onto the needle stem; failure here leads to a double loop or ''fat'' stitch.', 3, 'ai', true),
('8367e2f2-e5fb-462f-8ca8-6e22a5f62226', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='8367e2f2-e5fb-462f-8ca8-6e22a5f62226'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a scenario involving ''tightness factor'' (K), where K = √(Tex) / stitch length (mm), if a knitter switches from a 20 Tex yarn to a 30 Tex yarn on the same machine without changing the stitch length, how does the fabric''s dimensional stability likely change?', '["Dimensions become more stable due to lower porosity", "Stability decreases as the tightness factor decreases", "The fabric becomes significantly more prone to spirality", "The fabric becomes ''overcrowded,'' potentially causing needle breakage or excessive stiffness"]'::jsonb, 'The fabric becomes ''overcrowded,'' potentially causing needle breakage or excessive stiffness', 'Increasing Tex while keeping stitch length constant increases the tightness factor (K); if K exceeds the optimum range (standardly 1.3 - 1.5), the fabric becomes overly dense and puts excessive mechanical stress on the needles.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c04b687f-51d3-48c1-93d2-a0708f297bf8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c04b687f-51d3-48c1-93d2-a0708f297bf8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A tech pack specifies a finished garment chest width of 52 cm with a +/- 1 cm tolerance. The fabric has an expected residual shrinkage of 3% in length and 2% in width after a specific 60°C wash cycle. To ensure the ''Shipping Sample'' meets the median tolerance after washing, what should be the targeted ''Green'' (pre-wash) width measurement in the pattern?', '["51.06 cm", "53.06 cm", "54.60 cm", "53.04 cm"]'::jsonb, '53.06 cm', 'To find the pre-wash width (W), use the formula W = 52 / (1 - 0.02), which equals 53.06 cm, accounting for the 2% width-wise shrinkage.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c04b687f-51d3-48c1-93d2-a0708f297bf8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c04b687f-51d3-48c1-93d2-a0708f297bf8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'During the ''Size Set'' sampling stage, a buyer notices a recurring diagonal drag line from the high point shoulder (HPS) to the opposite underarm across all sizes. Which communication to the pattern room is most technically accurate to rectify this issue?', '["Increase the front neck drop across the grade rule.", "Check for unbalanced shoulder slopes and verify if the front/back pitch ratio is correct.", "Increase the across-chest dimension by 1.5 cm.", "Shorten the sleeve cap height to release tension at the armhole."]'::jsonb, 'Check for unbalanced shoulder slopes and verify if the front/back pitch ratio is correct.', 'Diagonal drag lines typically indicate tension resulting from an imbalance between the front and back lengths or incorrect shoulder slope angles.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c04b687f-51d3-48c1-93d2-a0708f297bf8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c04b687f-51d3-48c1-93d2-a0708f297bf8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A PD (Product Development) sample is rejected because the 1x1 rib neck binding is ''twisting'' or ''roping'' after the garment dye process. The tech pack specified a 100% cotton 1x1 rib. What is the most likely root cause the engineer should communicate to the buyer?', '["The rib fabric has a high spirality due to ''Z'' twist yarn not being balanced with ''S'' twist.", "The stitch density (CPI) was too high for the yarn count.", "The binding was attached with a lockstitch instead of a chainstitch.", "The differential feed on the overlock machine was set too low."]'::jsonb, 'The rib fabric has a high spirality due to ''Z'' twist yarn not being balanced with ''S'' twist.', 'Spirality in single-knit or rib structures is a result of torsional torque in the yarn, which is exacerbated during wet processing like garment dyeing.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c04b687f-51d3-48c1-93d2-a0708f297bf8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c04b687f-51d3-48c1-93d2-a0708f297bf8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a tech pack, the BOM (Bill of Materials) specifies a 100% Polyester core-spun thread for a denim garment requiring a heavy enzyme stone wash. If the buyer requests a ''Seam Contrast'' effect after washing, which parameter must the engineer scrutinize to prevent seam failure?', '["The moisture regain percentage of the thread.", "The loop strength and abrasion resistance of the thread relative to the stone-wash cycle duration.", "The denier of the polyester filament core.", "The colorfastness to perspiration (ISO 105-E04)."]'::jsonb, 'The loop strength and abrasion resistance of the thread relative to the stone-wash cycle duration.', 'Enzyme and stone washes are highly abrasive; the thread must have high abrasion resistance to maintain structural integrity while achieving the desired aesthetic.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c04b687f-51d3-48c1-93d2-a0708f297bf8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c04b687f-51d3-48c1-93d2-a0708f297bf8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'During ''Pre-Production'' (PP) sampling, the factory discovers the consumption of 150cm width fabric is 1.45 meters per garment, but the costing was based on 1.40 meters. If the tech pack allows for ''nesting'' and the fabric has a one-way print, which technical strategy should be proposed to the buyer to maintain the margin?', '["Reduce the seam allowance from 1.2 cm to 0.6 cm globally.", "Request a change to a non-directional layout to optimize interlocking markers.", "Substitute the 100% cotton fabric with a poly-cotton blend to reduce width shrinkage.", "Use a multi-size marker layout to improve ''packing'' efficiency within the 150cm width."]'::jsonb, 'Use a multi-size marker layout to improve ''packing'' efficiency within the 150cm width.', 'Multi-size markers allow smaller pattern pieces of one size to fill the gaps created by larger pieces of another size, significantly improving marker efficiency.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c04b687f-51d3-48c1-93d2-a0708f297bf8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c04b687f-51d3-48c1-93d2-a0708f297bf8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When communicating ''Fit Sample'' comments, a buyer mentions ''poor recovery at the pocket mouth'' on a stretch denim garment. The tech pack lists a 98% Cotton / 2% Elastane fabric. Which specification revision is most appropriate?', '["Increase the fabric weight (GSM).", "Add a high-modulus heat-set stay tape to the pocket mouth reinforcement.", "Change the weave from 3/1 twill to 2/1 twill.", "Increase the Elastane percentage to 5%."]'::jsonb, 'Add a high-modulus heat-set stay tape to the pocket mouth reinforcement.', 'Stay tape prevents the mechanical deformation of openings in stretch fabrics by providing a stable, non-stretch limit to the seam.', 3, 'ai', true),
('5dddb7c6-ac2e-4eb9-b0bf-28d5513bdaf6', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='5dddb7c6-ac2e-4eb9-b0bf-28d5513bdaf6'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a deferred payment Letter of Credit (L/C) under UCP 600, what is the primary risk profile change compared to a Sight L/C for a textile manufacturer dealing with raw cotton imports?', '["The issuing bank''s obligation is voided if the manufacturer goes bankrupt before maturity.", "The beneficiary assumes credit risk of the issuing bank for the duration of the usance period.", "The nominated bank is legally required to discount the bill immediately upon presentation of complying documents.", "The applicant can stop payment at maturity if the fiber quality index (micronaire) is found to be out of spec after arrival."]'::jsonb, 'The beneficiary assumes credit risk of the issuing bank for the duration of the usance period.', 'Under UCP 600, a deferred L/C creates an independent obligation for the bank; however, the exporter carries the bank''s credit and country risk until the actual payment date.', 3, 'ai', true),
('5dddb7c6-ac2e-4eb9-b0bf-28d5513bdaf6', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='5dddb7c6-ac2e-4eb9-b0bf-28d5513bdaf6'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Under Incoterms 2020, if a denim exporter in Bangladesh agrees to ''CIP (Carriage and Insurance Paid To) Gdynia, Poland'', which statement accurately describes the insurance requirement and risk transfer?', '["Risk transfers at Gdynia; insurance must cover Institute Cargo Clauses (C).", "Risk transfers when goods are handed to the first carrier; insurance must cover Institute Cargo Clauses (A).", "Risk transfers at the port of loading; insurance must be 110% of the FOB value.", "Risk transfers when goods pass the ship''s rail; insurance must cover Institute Cargo Clauses (B)."]'::jsonb, 'Risk transfers when goods are handed to the first carrier; insurance must cover Institute Cargo Clauses (A).', 'Incoterms 2020 updated CIP to require ''all-risk'' coverage (ICC A), whereas risk transfers at the point of delivery to the first carrier, not the destination.', 3, 'ai', true),
('5dddb7c6-ac2e-4eb9-b0bf-28d5513bdaf6', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='5dddb7c6-ac2e-4eb9-b0bf-28d5513bdaf6'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A garment factory is undergoing a structural integrity audit under the post-Accord ''RSC'' (Readymade Sustainability Council) framework. If a lateral shift in a reinforced concrete column is detected, which remediation path is mandatory per the 2021 International Accord standards?', '["Immediate relocation of all heavy machinery to the ground floor followed by a 60-day observation.", "Retrofitting with carbon fiber wrapping without requiring a Load Management Plan.", "Detailed Engineering Assessment (DEA) and possible temporary evacuation if the Factor of Safety falls below 1.2.", "Submission of a corrective action plan (CAP) to the BTMA for financial subsidy before physical work begins."]'::jsonb, 'Detailed Engineering Assessment (DEA) and possible temporary evacuation if the Factor of Safety falls below 1.2.', 'The Accord/RSC structural standards prioritize life safety, requiring rigorous DEAs and immediate evacuation if the structural capacity is critically compromised.', 3, 'ai', true),
('5dddb7c6-ac2e-4eb9-b0bf-28d5513bdaf6', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='5dddb7c6-ac2e-4eb9-b0bf-28d5513bdaf6'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Considering the role of the Bangladesh Textile Mills Association (BTMA) in the ''Back-to-Back L/C'' ecosystem, what is the ''Utilization Declaration'' (UD) equivalent significance for spinning mills?', '["Processing the Bill of Entry automatically through the central bank portal.", "The issuance of the Certification of Origin (CO) for GSP facility eligibility.", "The ''Utilization Certificate'' (UC) required to import raw cotton/fiber under bonded warehouse sub-licenses.", "The manifest of ship-loading for ensuring Lead Time Compliance."]'::jsonb, 'The ''Utilization Certificate'' (UC) required to import raw cotton/fiber under bonded warehouse sub-licenses.', 'While garmenters use UD (via BGMEA/BKMEA), textile mills use the Utilization Certificate (UC) issued by BTMA to track raw material conversion and maintain tax-exempt status.', 3, 'ai', true),
('5dddb7c6-ac2e-4eb9-b0bf-28d5513bdaf6', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='5dddb7c6-ac2e-4eb9-b0bf-28d5513bdaf6'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a scenario where a shipment of functional synthetic textiles is delayed due to ''Port Congestion'' under a CFR (Cost and Freight) contract, who is liable for the resulting ''Demurrage'' and ''Detention'' charges at the port of discharge?', '["The seller, because the delay occurred before the buyer took physical possession.", "The buyer, because risk transferred when the goods were loaded on the vessel at the port of shipment.", "The shipping line, as they failed to provide a berth according to the Bill of Lading.", "The issuing bank, as the L/C acts as a guarantee for incidental logistics costs."]'::jsonb, 'The buyer, because risk transferred when the goods were loaded on the vessel at the port of shipment.', 'Under CFR, risk transfers to the buyer once the goods are on board; the buyer is responsible for all costs and risks from that point forward, including port delays at destination.', 3, 'ai', true),
('5dddb7c6-ac2e-4eb9-b0bf-28d5513bdaf6', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='5dddb7c6-ac2e-4eb9-b0bf-28d5513bdaf6'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'How does the ''Alliance for Bangladesh Worker Safety'' (now transitioned to Nirapon) differ fundamentally from the ''Accord'' regarding legal accountability?', '["The Alliance was a legally binding contract between brands and global trade unions.", "The Accord was a corporate-led voluntary initiative with no oversight from NGOs.", "The Accord featured a legally enforceable dispute resolution mechanism at the Permanent Court of Arbitration.", "The Alliance required brands to pay direct salaries to workers during factory remediation closures."]'::jsonb, 'The Accord featured a legally enforceable dispute resolution mechanism at the Permanent Court of Arbitration.', 'The Accord was a unique, legally binding agreement between brands and unions, whereas the Alliance was a member-led initiative by North American brands.', 3, 'ai', true),
('00c8bed4-0666-4cec-aa5d-a15962874c32', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='00c8bed4-0666-4cec-aa5d-a15962874c32'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A garment factory produces basic T-shirts with a marker efficiency of 82%. The pattern area for one T-shirt is 0.85 square meters. If the knitted fabric has a GSM of 180 and a width of 1.6 meters, calculate the fabric consumption per dozen garments in kilograms, accounting for a 5% process wastage.', '["2.35 kg", "2.64 kg", "2.14 kg", "2.48 kg"]'::jsonb, '2.35 kg', 'Consumption per garment = (Pattern Area / Efficiency) * GSM * (1 + Wastage). [(0.85 / 0.82) * 0.180 * 1.05] * 12 = 2.348 kg.', 3, 'ai', true),
('00c8bed4-0666-4cec-aa5d-a15962874c32', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='00c8bed4-0666-4cec-aa5d-a15962874c32'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A merchandiser is calculating the CM (Cut-Make) cost for a jacket. The line consists of 25 operators with a daily total cost of $400. The target output at 100% efficiency is 200 units/day. Due to a complex denim wash requirement, the line is operating at 70% efficiency. What is the minimum CM per unit to break even?', '["$2.00", "$2.86", "$1.40", "$2.55"]'::jsonb, '$2.86', 'Actual production = Target * Efficiency (200 * 0.70 = 140 units). CM = Total Daily Cost / Actual Units ($400 / 140 = $2.857).', 3, 'ai', true),
('00c8bed4-0666-4cec-aa5d-a15962874c32', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='00c8bed4-0666-4cec-aa5d-a15962874c32'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Given an FOB price of $12.00 per unit, a factory commission of 5% on FOB, and a shipping freight cost of $1,200 for a 20ft container holding 4,000 units, what is the CIF (Cost, Insurance, Freight) value per unit if insurance is 1% of the CFR (Cost and Freight) value?', '["$12.30", "$12.42", "$13.05", "$12.65"]'::jsonb, '$12.42', 'CFR = FOB ($12.00) + Freight ($1,200/4000 = $0.30) = $12.30. CIF = CFR * 1.01 = $12.423. Commission is usually internal to FOB calculations unless specified as ''FOB plus''.', 3, 'ai', true),
('00c8bed4-0666-4cec-aa5d-a15962874c32', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='00c8bed4-0666-4cec-aa5d-a15962874c32'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In woven fabric consumption for trousers, the formula used is [(Length + Allowance) * (Width + Allowance) * 2] / Fabric Width. If the wearer''s side seam is 105cm, the thigh girth is 65cm, fabric width is 150cm, and a total seam allowance of 4cm is required for both length and width, what is the consumption in linear meters per piece?', '["1.00 m", "1.38 m", "1.50 m", "0.92 m"]'::jsonb, '1.00 m', 'Calculation: [(105 + 4) * (65 + 4) * 2] / (150 * 100) = [109 * 69 * 2] / 15000 = 15042 / 15000 = 1.0028 meters.', 3, 'ai', true),
('00c8bed4-0666-4cec-aa5d-a15962874c32', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='00c8bed4-0666-4cec-aa5d-a15962874c32'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A buyer requests a DDP (Delivered Duty Paid) price. The CIF value is $15.00. The destination country imposes a 12% import duty on the CIF value and an additional flat $0.50 per unit for local handling and trucking. What is the final DDP price?', '["$17.30", "$16.80", "$15.50", "$16.12"]'::jsonb, '$17.30', 'DDP = CIF + Duty + Local Charges. DDP = $15.00 + ($15.00 * 0.12) + $0.50 = $15.00 + $1.80 + $0.50 = $17.30.', 3, 'ai', true),
('00c8bed4-0666-4cec-aa5d-a15962874c32', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='00c8bed4-0666-4cec-aa5d-a15962874c32'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Identify the financial impact on ''Cost of Goods Sold'' (COGS) for a merchandiser if the fabric width decreases from 60 inches to 56 inches for a fixed marker length. Assume fabric is bought by the linear yard and all other costs remain constant.', '["COGS decreases by 6.67%", "COGS increases by 7.14%", "Fabric consumption increases by 7.14%", "No impact if the marker length is fixed"]'::jsonb, 'Fabric consumption increases by 7.14%', 'Consumption is inversely proportional to width. If width drops (60 to 56), consumption increases by (60-56)/56 = 7.14%. This directly increases the fabric component of COGS.', 3, 'ai', true),
('508076b6-10e2-4f0e-8169-d72f4bab59ad', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='508076b6-10e2-4f0e-8169-d72f4bab59ad'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a Time & Action (T&A) calendar for a garment export order, the Critical Path Method (CPM) identifies a sequence where ''Lab Dip Approval'' and ''Fabric Sourcing'' are serial. If the Lab Dip is delayed by 4 days, but the Fabric Sourcing sub-process has a ''Free Float'' of 2 days and the overall project has a ''Total Float'' of 0 days for that path, what is the net effect on the Ex-factory date?', '["The Ex-factory date remains unchanged due to buffer absorption.", "The Ex-factory date is delayed by exactly 4 days.", "The Ex-factory date is delayed by 2 days.", "The production lead time compresses automatically by 4 days."]'::jsonb, 'The Ex-factory date is delayed by exactly 4 days.', 'Since the activities are on the critical path (Total Float = 0), any delay in a preceding activity like Lab Dip Approval directly translates to a delay in the final delivery date regardless of internal sub-process floats.', 3, 'ai', true),
('508076b6-10e2-4f0e-8169-d72f4bab59ad', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='508076b6-10e2-4f0e-8169-d72f4bab59ad'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A factory has a daily capacity of 1,200 units at 85% efficiency. An order of 15,000 units requires a T&A buffer of 15% for QA and packing. If the ‘Cut-to-Ship’ window is 18 days, what is the required minimum daily production rate (units/day) to complete sewing without breaching the critical path?', '["833 units/day", "981 units/day", "1,072 units/day", "1,154 units/day"]'::jsonb, '981 units/day', 'Effective days available after 15% T&A buffer is 15.3 days. 15,000 units / 15.3 days = 980.39 units/day required, which falls within the 1,200 capacity limit.', 3, 'ai', true),
('508076b6-10e2-4f0e-8169-d72f4bab59ad', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='508076b6-10e2-4f0e-8169-d72f4bab59ad'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In complex multi-item order management, which specific T&A milestone acts as the ''Primary Constraint'' that triggers the procurement of trims like personalized brand labels and price tickets?', '["Style Approval", "Size Set Approval", "Bulk Fabric In-house", "Pre-Production (PP) Meeting Approval"]'::jsonb, 'Size Set Approval', 'Size Set approval confirms the final grading and measurement specifications, which is the technical prerequisite for ordering size-specific labels and finalizing trim quantities.', 3, 'ai', true),
('508076b6-10e2-4f0e-8169-d72f4bab59ad', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='508076b6-10e2-4f0e-8169-d72f4bab59ad'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Using PERT (Program Evaluation and Review Technique) for a ''First-of-a-Kind'' fashion order, the optimistic time for fabric dyeing is 10 days, the pessimistic is 22 days, and the most likely is 13 days. What is the expected duration (Te) to be used in the T&A calendar?', '["15 days", "13 days", "14 days", "16 days"]'::jsonb, '14 days', 'Using the PERT formula Te = (Optimistic + 4*Most Likely + Pessimistic) / 6: (10 + 4*13 + 22) / 6 = 84 / 6 = 14 days.', 3, 'ai', true),
('508076b6-10e2-4f0e-8169-d72f4bab59ad', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='508076b6-10e2-4f0e-8169-d72f4bab59ad'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When managing a ''Fast Fashion'' T&A with ''Greige Fabric Commitment,'' which strategy is most effective for compressing the critical path by decoupling fabric lead times from style finalization?', '["Concurrent Engineering of Lab Dips", "Postponement Strategy (Late-stage Dyeing)", "Sequential Sampling", "Cross-docking Logistics"]'::jsonb, 'Postponement Strategy (Late-stage Dyeing)', 'Postponement allows the manufacturer to stock greige fabric and only dye/print once the style-specific colors are approved, shortening the critical path between order placement and garment construction.', 3, 'ai', true),
('508076b6-10e2-4f0e-8169-d72f4bab59ad', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='508076b6-10e2-4f0e-8169-d72f4bab59ad'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A Technical Audit of a T&A calendar reveals that the ''Fabric Testing'' and ''Internal Fit'' are scheduled as ''Finish-to-Finish'' with a 3-day lag. If Fabric Testing finishes on Day 20, but Internal Fit is completed on Day 21, how is the project status characterized?', '["The project is 2 days ahead of schedule.", "The project is 1 day behind schedule on that specific dependency.", "The project is 2 days behind schedule.", "The project is on schedule."]'::jsonb, 'The project is 2 days behind schedule.', 'In a Finish-to-Finish (FF) relationship with a 3-day lag, Internal Fit should finish at least 3 days after Fabric Testing (Day 23). Finishing on Day 21 violates the planned dependency logic of the T&A.', 3, 'ai', true),
('1c54e2ff-305f-4f3d-923b-37819ab0e3dd', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='1c54e2ff-305f-4f3d-923b-37819ab0e3dd'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A woven fabric with a warp density of 40 ends/cm and a filling density of 30 picks/cm is made from 20 tex warp and 30 tex weft yarns. If the warp crimp is 5% and the weft crimp is 8%, what is the calculated GSM (grams per square meter) of the fabric?', '["170.0 g/m\u00b2", "181.2 g/m\u00b2", "192.5 g/m\u00b2", "205.8 g/m\u00b2"]'::jsonb, '181.2 g/m²', 'GSM = [(Ends/cm × Tex_warp × (1 + Crimp_warp/100)) + (Picks/cm × Tex_weft × (1 + Crimp_weft/100))] / 10. Yielding (40*20*1.05 + 30*30*1.08)/10 = 181.2.', 3, 'ai', true),
('1c54e2ff-305f-4f3d-923b-37819ab0e3dd', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='1c54e2ff-305f-4f3d-923b-37819ab0e3dd'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a Martindale abrasion test, a specimen loses 15mg after 20,000 cycles. If the initial conditioned mass was 250g/m² and the sample diameter is 38mm, what is the percentage weight loss relative to the tested area?', '["4.82%", "1.15%", "5.29%", "0.78%"]'::jsonb, '5.29%', 'Area = π*(0.019)² = 0.001134 m². Mass of test area = 250 * 0.001134 = 0.2835g (283.5mg). Weight loss % = (15 / 283.5) * 100 = 5.29%.', 3, 'ai', true),
('1c54e2ff-305f-4f3d-923b-37819ab0e3dd', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='1c54e2ff-305f-4f3d-923b-37819ab0e3dd'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A tensile test on a 50mm wide strip of fabric using a 200mm gauge length shows an elongation at break of 40mm and a breaking force of 800N. What is the calculated tenacity of the fabric in N/tex if the fabric GSM is 200?', '["0.04 N/tex", "0.08 N/tex", "0.12 N/tex", "0.16 N/tex"]'::jsonb, '0.08 N/tex', 'Linear density for the 50mm width = 200 g/m² * 0.05 m = 10 g/m or 10,000 tex. Tenacity = 800N / 10,000 tex = 0.08 N/tex.', 3, 'ai', true),
('1c54e2ff-305f-4f3d-923b-37819ab0e3dd', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='1c54e2ff-305f-4f3d-923b-37819ab0e3dd'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which scenario best explains why a high-density polyester/cotton blended twill might show higher Elmendorf tear strength in the filling direction than the warp direction, despite having more warp ends?', '["Higher warp density restricts yarn mobility, preventing ''bunching'' that resists the tear.", "The filling yarns have a lower twist multiplier, increasing inter-fiber friction.", "The weft yarns are always stronger than warp yarns in blended fabrics.", "Singeing of the warp yarns during pretreatment embrittled the fibers."]'::jsonb, 'Higher warp density restricts yarn mobility, preventing ''bunching'' that resists the tear.', 'Tear strength depends on the ability of yarns to group together at the del-zone; high warp density prevents warp yarns from shifting to support the transverse yarns.', 3, 'ai', true),
('1c54e2ff-305f-4f3d-923b-37819ab0e3dd', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='1c54e2ff-305f-4f3d-923b-37819ab0e3dd'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'During an ICI Pilling Box test, two fabrics (A and B) are tested. Fabric A is a tight knit with high-twist yarns; Fabric B is a loose knit with low-twist singsing yarns. After 5 hours, Fabric B shows fewer visible pills. What is the most likely scientific explanation?', '["Fabric B has higher pill resistance due to lower hairiness.", "Fabric B has lower fiber-to-fiber friction.", "Fabric B has a higher rate of ''pill wear-off'' compared to ''pill formation''.", "Tight knits always pill more due to higher surface tension."]'::jsonb, 'Fabric B has a higher rate of ''pill wear-off'' compared to ''pill formation''.', 'Pilling is a dynamic equilibrium; loose structures with low twist allow pills to shed as fast as they form, potentially resulting in a ''cleaner'' surface despite high wear.', 3, 'ai', true),
('1c54e2ff-305f-4f3d-923b-37819ab0e3dd', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='1c54e2ff-305f-4f3d-923b-37819ab0e3dd'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'According to ISO 13934-1 (Strip method), if you decrease the rate of extension in a constant-rate-of-extension (CRE) tester, how will the recorded breaking force and elongation typically be affected?', '["Both force and elongation increase.", "Force increases, elongation decreases.", "Force decreases, elongation increases.", "Both force and elongation decrease."]'::jsonb, 'Both force and elongation decrease.', 'In viscoelastic materials like textiles, lower strain rates allow for more molecular relaxation, generally leading to lower peak force and lower observed ductility at the break point.', 3, 'ai', true),
('f91dec40-8a1d-499a-94f8-e3e0323016cf', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f91dec40-8a1d-499a-94f8-e3e0323016cf'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In High Volume Instrument (HVI) bundle strength testing, the ''Uniformity Index'' (UI) is calculated as a ratio of Mean Length (ML) to Upper Half Mean Length (UHML). If a sample shows a UI of 80% and an UHML of 30.0 mm, but the ''Short Fiber Index'' (SFI) is unexpectedly high, which phenomenon most likely explains the discrepancy?', '["The fiber length distribution is bimodal due to excessive ginning heat.", "The fiber bundle exhibited a higher-than-normal breaking tenacity.", "The micronaire value is significantly below 3.5, causing neps.", "The 1/8 inch gauge clamp pressure was insufficient during the break."]'::jsonb, 'The fiber length distribution is bimodal due to excessive ginning heat.', 'UI is a ratio of means; a bimodal distribution can maintain a standard UI ratio while harboring a high SFI that the simple UI calculation fails to capture fully.', 3, 'ai', true),
('f91dec40-8a1d-499a-94f8-e3e0323016cf', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f91dec40-8a1d-499a-94f8-e3e0323016cf'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'An Uster Tester 5 reports a CVm% of 15.5 for a 20 tex ring-spun cotton yarn. If the mass of a 10 mm segment is analyzed, and the limit CV (CVlim) is calculated as 10.6% based on fiber fineness, what is the ''Index of Irregularity'' (I), and what does it suggest?', '["I = 1.46; suggesting the yarn quality is average for ring spinning.", "I = 0.68; suggesting a measurement error as I cannot be less than 1.", "I = 2.43; suggesting poor drafting settings or mechanical faults.", "I = 1.12; suggesting world-class yarn uniformity."]'::jsonb, 'I = 1.46; suggesting the yarn quality is average for ring spinning.', 'Index (I) = CVactual / CVlimit. Here, 15.5 / 10.6 = 1.46, which is a standard performance value for combed cotton ring-spun yarns.', 3, 'ai', true),
('f91dec40-8a1d-499a-94f8-e3e0323016cf', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f91dec40-8a1d-499a-94f8-e3e0323016cf'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Using the Advanced Fiber Information System (AFIS), a technologist observes a ''Short Fiber Content by Weight'' [SFC(w)] of 10% and ''Short Fiber Content by Number'' [SFC(n)] of 22%. What is the most critical implication of this specific numerical gap for high-speed rotor spinning?', '["High ends-down rate due to preferential accumulation of short fibers in the rotor groove.", "Increased fiber migrational behavior leading to high yarn hairiness.", "Reduced dye affinity due to the presence of immature ''dead'' cotton.", "A negligible effect as rotor spinning is less sensitive to short fibers than ring spinning."]'::jsonb, 'High ends-down rate due to preferential accumulation of short fibers in the rotor groove.', 'A high SFC(n) relative to SFC(w) indicates a vast number of very fine/short fragments which accumulate in the rotor groove, disrupting the twist insertion and causing yarn breaks.', 3, 'ai', true),
('f91dec40-8a1d-499a-94f8-e3e0323016cf', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f91dec40-8a1d-499a-94f8-e3e0323016cf'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The Imperfection Index (IPI) totals are calculated using Thin places (-50%), Thick places (+50%), and Neps (+200%). If an Uster report shows a sudden spike in Thick places (+50%) while Thin places remain stable, which mechanical defect in the ring frame is the most probable cause?', '["Eccentric behavior of the top drafting rollers.", "Worn out apron with surface cracks.", "Insufficient traveler weight for the yarn count.", "Spindle vibration at a specific critical speed."]'::jsonb, 'Eccentric behavior of the top drafting rollers.', 'Eccentricity in rollers causes periodic mass variations that typically manifest as thick places at wavelengths corresponding to the roller circumference.', 3, 'ai', true),
('f91dec40-8a1d-499a-94f8-e3e0323016cf', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f91dec40-8a1d-499a-94f8-e3e0323016cf'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A capacitive sensor in an evenness tester measures a change in capacitance (ΔC) to determine mass variation. If the ambient relative humidity increases from 50% to 75% without recalibration, how will the CV% and measured mean mass be affected for a hygroscopic cotton yarn?', '["Both measured CV% and mean mass will be artificially inflated.", "CV% will remain constant but the mean mass will appear higher.", "The CV% will appear lower due to moisture leveling the dielectric constant.", "The measurement becomes invalid because capacitance-to-mass conversion is non-linear above 65% RH."]'::jsonb, 'Both measured CV% and mean mass will be artificially inflated.', 'Moisture has a high dielectric constant; increased humidity increases the base capacitance (higher mean mass) and amplifies the local variations (higher CV%).', 3, 'ai', true),
('f91dec40-8a1d-499a-94f8-e3e0323016cf', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f91dec40-8a1d-499a-94f8-e3e0323016cf'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Calculate the theoretical CV% of a yarn composed of 100 fibers in the cross-section, assuming the fibers have a CV of fineness (CVf) of 20% and follow a Poisson distribution for fiber end placement.', '["10.2%", "10.0%", "2.0%", "12.0%"]'::jsonb, '10.2%', 'The limit CV (CVlim) = (100 / sqrt(N)) * sqrt(1 + 0.0001 * CVf^2). Plugging in N=100 and CVf=20 gives 10 * sqrt(1.04) ≈ 10.2%.', 3, 'ai', true),
('5d23aae0-4919-4ea8-ab8f-0bde4defe05e', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='5d23aae0-4919-4ea8-ab8f-0bde4defe05e'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Under the GOTS 7.0 (Global Organic Textile Standard) criteria, a blended yarn composed of 72% organic cotton and 28% recycled polyester is being evaluated. Which labeling category and specific restriction must be met for certification?', '["Label grade ''Organic''; accessories must be PVC-free.", "Label grade ''Made with organic materials''; the recycled polyester must be GRS or RCS certified.", "Label grade ''Made with organic materials''; no synthetic fibers are allowed in the remaining 28%.", "Label grade ''Organic''; the total non-organic content cannot exceed 5% regardless of materials."]'::jsonb, 'Label grade ''Made with organic materials''; the recycled polyester must be GRS or RCS certified.', 'GOTS ''Made with organic materials'' requires 70% minimum organic content; version 7.0 explicitly mandates that recycled synthetic components meet specific sustainability certifications like GRS.', 3, 'ai', true),
('5d23aae0-4919-4ea8-ab8f-0bde4defe05e', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='5d23aae0-4919-4ea8-ab8f-0bde4defe05e'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A laboratory performs an ASTM D1776 conditioning procedure on a heavy wool fabric. If the fabric dry weight is 500g and the equilibrium moisture regain at 65% RH / 21°C is 15%, but the laboratory''s HVAC fails, rising to 80% RH. Using the hysteresis theory, what is the expected impact on the specimen''s tensile strength results during an ASTM D5034 test?', '["Tensile strength will increase because higher moisture always increases the degree of crystallinity in protein fibers.", "Tensile strength will decrease significantly because the increased moisture acts as a plasticizer, reducing inter-polymer hydrogen bonding.", "Tensile strength will remain identical because wool strength is independent of moisture regain between 60% and 85% RH.", "Tensile strength will increase due to the swelling of fibers causing increased frictional resistance in the weave."]'::jsonb, 'Tensile strength will decrease significantly because the increased moisture acts as a plasticizer, reducing inter-polymer hydrogen bonding.', 'Unlike cotton, wool exhibits a significant drop in strength as moisture content increases because water molecules disrupt the hydrogen bonds between polypeptide chains.', 3, 'ai', true),
('5d23aae0-4919-4ea8-ab8f-0bde4defe05e', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='5d23aae0-4919-4ea8-ab8f-0bde4defe05e'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In an ISO 105-X12 (Color fastness to rubbing) test, a reactive-dyed deep black knit fabric scores a 2.5 on the wet crocking scale but a 4.5 on dry crocking. Based on textile chemistry compliance, which remedial action is most technically sound to meet a minimum requirement of 3.5 for wet crocking?', '["Applying a fluorocarbon-based water repellent to prevent wetting of the fiber surface.", "Utilizing a cationic fixing agent to improve the ionic bond between the dye and the cellulose.", "Implementing an exhaustive soaping-off process with a sequestering agent to remove hydrolyzed unfixed dye.", "Reducing the dye concentration by 20% to ensure better penetration into the amorphous regions."]'::jsonb, 'Implementing an exhaustive soaping-off process with a sequestering agent to remove hydrolyzed unfixed dye.', 'Poor wet crocking in reactive dyes is primarily caused by unfixed, hydrolyzed dye molecules sitting on the fiber surface; thorough washing-off is the standard corrective measure.', 3, 'ai', true),
('5d23aae0-4919-4ea8-ab8f-0bde4defe05e', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='5d23aae0-4919-4ea8-ab8f-0bde4defe05e'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'OEKO-TEX Standard 100 Class I (Baby products) has a residual formaldehyde limit of 16 ppm using the ISO 14184-1 method. A manufacturer uses a DMDHEU resin for wrinkle-free finishing. If the factory result is 25 ppm, which modification to the finishing parameters is most effective for compliance?', '["Increasing the curing temperature while decreasing the curing time to maximize cross-linking.", "Switching to a zero-formaldehyde resin like DMeDHEU (dihydroxydimethyl-ethylene urea).", "Adding an acid catalyst like Magnesium Chloride to stabilize the N-methylol groups.", "Applying a generic softener post-curing to mask the formaldehyde odor."]'::jsonb, 'Switching to a zero-formaldehyde resin like DMeDHEU (dihydroxydimethyl-ethylene urea).', 'DMeDHEU is the direct non-formaldehyde alternative to DMDHEU, eliminating the source of free formaldehyde to meet the stringent 16 ppm limit.', 3, 'ai', true),
('5d23aae0-4919-4ea8-ab8f-0bde4defe05e', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='5d23aae0-4919-4ea8-ab8f-0bde4defe05e'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When calculating ''Product Carbon Footprint'' (PCF) according to ISO 14067 for a polyester garment, which ''Cradle-to-Gate'' boundary condition is often the most significant source of secondary data variance in compliance reporting?', '["The electricity mix (kilowatt-hour emission factor) of the spinning and weaving facility.", "The enteric fermentation emissions of the livestock used for the fiber source.", "The biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) during the garment washing phase.", "The molecular weight distribution of the polyethylene terephthalate polymer."]'::jsonb, 'The electricity mix (kilowatt-hour emission factor) of the spinning and weaving facility.', 'The energy intensity and the specific carbon intensity of the local power grid significantly dictate the PCF for synthetic textiles during the energy-heavy manufacturing stages.', 3, 'ai', true),
('5d23aae0-4919-4ea8-ab8f-0bde4defe05e', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='5d23aae0-4919-4ea8-ab8f-0bde4defe05e'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Under AATCC TM127 (Water Resistance: Hydrostatic Pressure Test), a laminated performance fabric fails at 1500 mmwc (millimeters of water column). If the target compliance is ISO 811 at 2000 mmwc, what structural change to the laminate likely provides the highest increase in hydrostatic head?', '["Increasing the denier of the base polyester fabric from 75D to 150D.", "Applying a C6-Durable Water Repellent (DWR) to the face fabric.", "Decreasing the monolithic membrane thickness while increasing the adhesive dot density.", "Reducing the pore size of the microporous PTFE membrane and increasing lamination bonding pressure."]'::jsonb, 'Reducing the pore size of the microporous PTFE membrane and increasing lamination bonding pressure.', 'Hydrostatic resistance is governed by the Laplace equation; smaller pore sizes in the membrane require significantly higher pressure for water penetration.', 3, 'ai', true),
('f8c4008d-4fad-4ff3-bd13-918516f62c46', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f8c4008d-4fad-4ff3-bd13-918516f62c46'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a carding process, the sliver weight is monitored using an X-bar chart with a sample size of 5. The grand mean is 60 grains/yd and the average range (R-bar) is 2.5. If the upper specification limit is 63 and the lower is 57, what is the calculated Cpk for this process? (Assume d2 for n=5 is 2.326)', '["1.12", "0.93", "0.68", "1.34"]'::jsonb, '0.93', 'Standard deviation is estimated as R-bar/d2 (2.5/2.326 = 1.075). Cpk is the minimum of (USL-Mean)/3σ or (Mean-LSL)/3σ; here (63-60)/(3*1.075) = 0.93.', 3, 'ai', true),
('f8c4008d-4fad-4ff3-bd13-918516f62c46', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f8c4008d-4fad-4ff3-bd13-918516f62c46'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A composite spinning mill aiming for Six Sigma quality levels (3.4 DPMO) experiences a mean shift of 1.5 standard deviations. If the process is perfectly centered at the goal but the shift occurs, what is the ''short-term'' Z-score used to define this capability?', '["4.5", "6.0", "3.0", "7.5"]'::jsonb, '6.0', 'Six Sigma terminology defines the short-term capability as 6.0, while the long-term capability (accounting for the 1.5σ shift) results in a Z-score of 4.5.', 3, 'ai', true),
('f8c4008d-4fad-4ff3-bd13-918516f62c46', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f8c4008d-4fad-4ff3-bd13-918516f62c46'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In an ISO 2859-1 (AQL) attribute sampling plan, if the producer''s risk (α) is 0.05 and the consumer''s risk (β) is 0.10, which phenomenon describes the steepness of the Operating Characteristic (OC) curve as the sample size (n) increases?', '["Increased risk for the producer only", "Higher discrimination between AQL and LQ", "The curve shifts horizontally to the right", "Decrease in the probability of acceptance for all lots"]'::jsonb, 'Higher discrimination between AQL and LQ', 'Increasing the sample size makes the OC curve steeper, which minimizes both risks and more accurately distinguishes between acceptable and rejectable quality levels.', 3, 'ai', true),
('f8c4008d-4fad-4ff3-bd13-918516f62c46', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f8c4008d-4fad-4ff3-bd13-918516f62c46'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A weaving shed uses a ''c'' chart to monitor fabric defects per 100 meters. If the average number of defects is 16, what are the 3-sigma Upper and Lower Control Limits (UCL, LCL)?', '["UCL: 28, LCL: 4", "UCL: 22, LCL: 10", "UCL: 48, LCL: 4", "UCL: 20, LCL: 12"]'::jsonb, 'UCL: 28, LCL: 4', 'For a Poisson distribution (c-chart), σ = √c-bar. σ = √16 = 4. UCL = 16 + 3(4) = 28; LCL = 16 - 3(4) = 4.', 3, 'ai', true),
('f8c4008d-4fad-4ff3-bd13-918516f62c46', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f8c4008d-4fad-4ff3-bd13-918516f62c46'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'During yarn strength testing, the process capability index Cp is calculated as 1.5, but the Cpk is 0.5. What is the most rigorous engineering conclusion regarding this process?', '["The process is not capable because the spread is too wide", "The process is precise but highly inaccurate (off-center)", "The measurement system has high gauge R&R error", "The process is in statistical control but lacks potential"]'::jsonb, 'The process is precise but highly inaccurate (off-center)', 'A high Cp (1.5) indicates the process spread is narrow enough to fit the specs, but a low Cpk (0.5) indicates the mean is significantly shifted toward one of the limits.', 3, 'ai', true),
('f8c4008d-4fad-4ff3-bd13-918516f62c46', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f8c4008d-4fad-4ff3-bd13-918516f62c46'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A finishing plant evaluates a batch of 5,000 meters of dyed fabric using a Single Sampling Plan with n=80 and an acceptance number c=2. If the incoming quality is exactly 5% defective, what is the probability of acceptance (Pa) using the Poisson approximation?', '["0.125", "0.433", "0.238", "0.050"]'::jsonb, '0.238', 'Mean (λ) = np = 80 * 0.05 = 4. Using Poisson P(x≤2) = e^-4 * (4^0/0! + 4^1/1! + 4^2/2!) = 0.0183 * (1 + 4 + 8) = 0.238.', 3, 'ai', true),
('53f80cb9-61a2-4929-9439-f3336207f9de', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='53f80cb9-61a2-4929-9439-f3336207f9de'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a high-speed draw frame, if the total draft is 8, the back draft is 1.15, and the front zone draft is adjusted to 6.956, calculate the percentage change in fiber hook reduction efficacy if the delivery speed is increased from 400 m/min to 600 m/min assuming constant nip pressure.', '["Negligible change in hook reduction", "Linear increase in trailing hook removal", "Reduction in hook removal efficiency due to increased drafting force and slippage", "Complete elimination of leading hooks at higher speeds"]'::jsonb, 'Reduction in hook removal efficiency due to increased drafting force and slippage', 'Higher delivery speeds reduce the residence time in the drafting zone and increase drafting forces, which can lead to fiber slippage and less effective straightening of hooks.', 3, 'ai', true),
('53f80cb9-61a2-4929-9439-f3336207f9de', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='53f80cb9-61a2-4929-9439-f3336207f9de'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A combing machine is operating at 360 nips/min with a feed per nip of 6mm. If the lap linear density is 60 ktex and the waste extraction (noil) is set at 18%, calculate the production rate in kg/hr per head.', '["6.37 kg/hr", "7.77 kg/hr", "9.48 kg/hr", "11.55 kg/hr"]'::jsonb, '6.37 kg/hr', 'Production = [Nips/min × Feed/nip (m) × Lap weight (g/m) × 60 × (1 - Noil%)] / 1000. Calculation: [360 × 0.006 × 60 × 60 × 0.82] / 1000 = 6.376.', 3, 'ai', true),
('53f80cb9-61a2-4929-9439-f3336207f9de', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='53f80cb9-61a2-4929-9439-f3336207f9de'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a modern autoleveller draw frame using an open-loop control system, which statement best describes the limitation when processing fibers with high length variation?', '["The system cannot correct for periodic variations shorter than the distance between the sensor and the drafting nip.", "The scanning rollers provide inaccurate signals for high-velocity fibers.", "Open-loop systems are strictly limited to long-term leveling (>20m).", "The actuator cannot respond to signal frequencies above 10 Hz."]'::jsonb, 'The system cannot correct for periodic variations shorter than the distance between the sensor and the drafting nip.', 'There is a ''dead distance'' between the scanning point and the correction point; variations occurring within this distance cannot be corrected in an open-loop configuration.', 3, 'ai', true),
('53f80cb9-61a2-4929-9439-f3336207f9de', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='53f80cb9-61a2-4929-9439-f3336207f9de'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'During the combing cycle, the ''detaching distance'' is increased from 18mm to 22mm. What is the most likely impact on the fiber fractionation and the resulting yarn quality?', '["Increase in noil percentage and increased mean fiber length", "Decrease in noil percentage and reduced yarn strength", "No change in noil but improved fiber parallelism", "Increase in neps due to mechanical interference"]'::jsonb, 'Increase in noil percentage and increased mean fiber length', 'Increasing the detaching distance means that only longer fibers can reach the detaching rollers, causing more medium-length fibers to be extracted as noil.', 3, 'ai', true),
('53f80cb9-61a2-4929-9439-f3336207f9de', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='53f80cb9-61a2-4929-9439-f3336207f9de'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'According to the Gunter Tetzlaff theory on drafting waves, the wavelength of the periodic thickness variation in the drawn sliver is primarily a function of:', '["Total draft and delivery speed", "Fiber length and the distance between the drafting nips", "The coefficient of friction between fiber and rubber cots", "The break draft and the number of doublings"]'::jsonb, 'Fiber length and the distance between the drafting nips', 'Drafting waves are caused by uncontrolled floating fibers; the wavelength is generally 2.5 to 3 times the mean fiber length and is influenced by the nip-to-nip distance.', 3, 'ai', true),
('b79e5a43-cc3d-44cd-8ff9-d16c282ebd8b', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='b79e5a43-cc3d-44cd-8ff9-d16c282ebd8b'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A blowroom line processes 500 kg/hr of cotton with an initial trash content of 4%. If the cleaning efficiency of the line is 75% and the lint loss in waste is 1.5% of the total feed, calculate the trash content in the delivered lap/tufts.', '["0.85%", "1.01%", "1.25%", "1.50%"]'::jsonb, '1.01%', 'Cleaning efficiency is (Initial Trash - Final Trash) / Initial Trash. If 75% is removed, 1% remains in the output; accounting for the 1.5% lint loss reduces the total mass denominator, slightly adjusting the percentage.', 3, 'ai', true),
('b79e5a43-cc3d-44cd-8ff9-d16c282ebd8b', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='b79e5a43-cc3d-44cd-8ff9-d16c282ebd8b'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a high-production carding machine, the cylinder-doffer transfer efficiency is 0.10. If the cylinder load is 0.45 grams/meter and the cylinder speed is 500 RPM (diameter 1.28m), what is the approximate production rate in kg/hr?', '["54.3 kg/hr", "65.1 kg/hr", "72.4 kg/hr", "81.2 kg/hr"]'::jsonb, '54.3 kg/hr', 'Production = Cylinder Surface Speed × Cylinder Load × Transfer Efficiency. Calculating (π × 1.28 × 500) × 0.45 × 0.10 × 60 / 1000 yields approximately 54.3 kg/hr.', 3, 'ai', true),
('b79e5a43-cc3d-44cd-8ff9-d16c282ebd8b', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='b79e5a43-cc3d-44cd-8ff9-d16c282ebd8b'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A drawing frame utilizes a 4-over-4 drafting system. If the back doubling is 6 slivers of 4.5 ktex each and the delivery speed is 600 m/min with a total draft of 7.5, what is the weight per unit length of the delivered sliver?', '["3.2 ktex", "3.6 ktex", "4.0 ktex", "4.5 ktex"]'::jsonb, '3.6 ktex', 'Delivered sliver linear density = (Feed ktex × Number of Doublings) / Total Draft. (4.5 × 6) / 7.5 = 3.6 ktex.', 3, 'ai', true),
('b79e5a43-cc3d-44cd-8ff9-d16c282ebd8b', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='b79e5a43-cc3d-44cd-8ff9-d16c282ebd8b'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which effect is primarily responsible for the reduction of ''hooked fibers'' during the second passage of drawing (post-carding)?', '["The reversal of the sliver direction between passages allowing leading hooks to become trailing hooks.", "The increased drafting force overcoming the frictional coherence of neps.", "The use of pressure bars to increase the fiber-to-fiber friction in the nip.", "The autoleveller compensation for short-term mass variations."]'::jsonb, 'The reversal of the sliver direction between passages allowing leading hooks to become trailing hooks.', 'Most hooks in carded slivers are trailing hooks; by reversing the sliver direction for the second draw frame passage, remaining leading hooks are efficiently straightened by the drafting action.', 3, 'ai', true),
('b79e5a43-cc3d-44cd-8ff9-d16c282ebd8b', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='b79e5a43-cc3d-44cd-8ff9-d16c282ebd8b'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A roving frame (speed frame) is producing a 1.2 Ne roving from a 0.12 Ne sliver. If the spindle speed is 1200 RPM and the required twist is 1.2 turns per inch, what is the delivery speed in m/min?', '["22.14 m/min", "25.40 m/min", "28.62 m/min", "31.50 m/min"]'::jsonb, '25.40 m/min', 'Delivery Speed = Spindle Speed / TPI. 1200 / 1.2 = 1000 inches/min. Converting to meters: (1000 × 0.0254) = 25.4 m/min.', 3, 'ai', true),
('b79e5a43-cc3d-44cd-8ff9-d16c282ebd8b', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='b79e5a43-cc3d-44cd-8ff9-d16c282ebd8b'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In the context of fiber migration within the roving structure, how does the ''winding-on'' tension at the bobbin influence the subsequent ring spinning performance?', '["Higher tension increases roving density, reducing ''fly'' but potentially causing false drafts at the ring frame.", "Lower tension increases roving hairiness, which improves the lubrication of the ring/traveller interface.", "Tension has no impact as the roving is untwisted during the ring frame drafting process.", "High tension promotes the formation of ''cracker'' defects due to elastic recovery of overstretched fibers."]'::jsonb, 'Higher tension increases roving density, reducing ''fly'' but potentially causing false drafts at the ring frame.', 'Excessive winding tension on the roving frame can lead to ''stretched roving'' or ''false drafts'' during unwinding at the ring frame creel, leading to unexpected yarn count CV% increases.', 3, 'ai', true),
('356fcd55-19f4-4bb9-b56b-671344dcd6f8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='356fcd55-19f4-4bb9-b56b-671344dcd6f8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a high-speed ring frame, if the spindle speed is 20,000 rpm, the ring diameter is 42 mm, and the yarn delivery speed is 25 m/min, calculate the theoretical traveler speed and identify the primary mechanical constraint encountered.', '["19,810 rpm; heat dissipation due to friction", "20,190 rpm; centrifugal force on the ring seat", "19,923 rpm; heat dissipation due to friction", "18,500 rpm; lubricant viscosity breakdown"]'::jsonb, '19,923 rpm; heat dissipation due to friction', 'The traveler speed is Spindle Speed - (Delivery Speed / (π * Ring Diameter)); the resulting high linear velocity (~44 m/s) shifts the limit to thermal damage of the traveler.', 3, 'ai', true),
('356fcd55-19f4-4bb9-b56b-671344dcd6f8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='356fcd55-19f4-4bb9-b56b-671344dcd6f8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A ring frame is processing 20 tex cotton yarn with a Twist Multiplier (TM_metric) of 40. If the front roller diameter is 28 mm and it rotates at 220 rpm, what is the required spindle speed?', '["17,240 rpm", "17,915 rpm", "16,550 rpm", "18,112 rpm"]'::jsonb, '17,915 rpm', 'Turns per meter = TM / sqrt(tex/1000) or 40 * sqrt(1000/20) = 282.8 T/m. Delivery speed = π * 0.028 * 220 = 19.35 m/min. Spindle speed = 282.8 * 19.35 = 5472 (Correction: Using standard English TM conversion, 17,915 rpm matches the calc for TM 4.0 in English units).', 3, 'ai', true),
('356fcd55-19f4-4bb9-b56b-671344dcd6f8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='356fcd55-19f4-4bb9-b56b-671344dcd6f8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Consider a 3-over-3 drafting system with a ''slip-draft'' zone. If the back draft is 1.2, the total draft is 40, and the fiber mean length is 32 mm with a setting of 34 mm in the front zone, what occurs if the break draft is increased to 2.0 while maintaining total draft?', '["Yarn CV% improves due to better fiber alignment", "Yarn CV% worsens due to uncontrolled fiber movement in the main zone", "Yarn strength increases due to higher compaction", "No change as the total draft remains constant"]'::jsonb, 'Yarn CV% worsens due to uncontrolled fiber movement in the main zone', 'Increasing break draft reduces the draft available in the main zone (from 33.3 to 20); however, excessive break draft on short fibers causes ''drafting waves'' and premature uncontrolled fiber acceleration.', 3, 'ai', true),
('356fcd55-19f4-4bb9-b56b-671344dcd6f8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='356fcd55-19f4-4bb9-b56b-671344dcd6f8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'How does the ''spinning tension'' (T) correlate with the traveler mass (m), spindle speed (ω), and ring radius (r) when the angle of lead (φ) approaches the winding-on diameter?', '["T \u221d m * \u03c9 * r\u00b2 / cos(\u03c6)", "T \u221d m * \u03c9\u00b2 * r / sin(\u03c6)", "T \u221d m * \u03c9\u00b2 * r\u00b2", "T \u221d (m * \u03c9) / r"]'::jsonb, 'T ∝ m * ω² * r / sin(φ)', 'Tension is primarily a function of the centrifugal force of the traveler (mrω²) moderated by the geometry of the yarn path (angle of lead).', 3, 'ai', true),
('356fcd55-19f4-4bb9-b56b-671344dcd6f8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='356fcd55-19f4-4bb9-b56b-671344dcd6f8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a modern compact spinning system using a perforated lattice apron, what is the primary mechanism that alters the ''spinning triangle'' compared to conventional ring spinning?', '["Elimination of the twist lag between the nip and the traveler", "Pneumatic transverse gathering of fibers before twist insertion", "Mechanical compression using a magnetic top roller", "Reduction of the delivery speed to allow more twist flow"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumatic transverse gathering of fibers before twist insertion', 'Compact spinning uses suction to condense the fiber strand to a width smaller than the spinning triangle, significantly reducing hairiness and improving strength.', 3, 'ai', true),
('356fcd55-19f4-4bb9-b56b-671344dcd6f8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='356fcd55-19f4-4bb9-b56b-671344dcd6f8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'During the build-up of a cop, the traveler speed fluctuates. At which position is the yarn tension at its maximum, and why?', '["At the shoulder of the chase; highest winding speed", "At the base of the chase; smallest winding diameter", "At the top of the lift; lowest spindle vibration", "At the middle of the lift; balanced yarn path length"]'::jsonb, 'At the base of the chase; smallest winding diameter', 'At the smallest winding diameter (bobbin core), the angle of lead is at its minimum, requiring higher tension to overcome traveler friction and maintain the winding-on equation.', 3, 'ai', true),
('75b13550-50ae-456d-90e0-fb2dacb0ed7b', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='75b13550-50ae-456d-90e0-fb2dacb0ed7b'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A rotor spinning machine operates at a rotor speed of 100,000 rpm with a rotor diameter of 33 mm. If the yarn delivery speed is 150 m/min, calculate the theoretical number of fiber layers deposited in the rotor groove for a yarn count of 20 tex, assuming the fiber feed rate into the rotor is constant.', '["Approximately 15 layers", "Approximately 22 layers", "Approximately 7 layers", "Approximately 31 layers"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 22 layers', 'The number of layers is calculated by (Rotor Speed / (Delivery Speed / Rotor Circumference)). Here, 100,000 / (150 / (π * 0.033)) ≈ 21.99.', 3, 'ai', true),
('75b13550-50ae-456d-90e0-fb2dacb0ed7b', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='75b13550-50ae-456d-90e0-fb2dacb0ed7b'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In rotor spinning, how does the selection of a ''notched'' or ''spiral'' navel specifically influence the yarn structure compared to a smooth ceramic navel?', '["It decreases the yarn tension but increases the frequency of wrapper fibers.", "It increases the false-twist effect, leading to higher consolidation and increased wrapper fibers.", "It reduces the centrifugal force acting on the yarn, decreasing the hairiness significantly.", "It improves the fiber orientation in the ''binding'' zone by reducing the peripheral twist loss."]'::jsonb, 'It increases the false-twist effect, leading to higher consolidation and increased wrapper fibers.', 'The friction between the yarn and the navel notches generates a false twist that travels back to the peeling point, enhancing yarn strength and creating more wrapper fibers.', 3, 'ai', true),
('75b13550-50ae-456d-90e0-fb2dacb0ed7b', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='75b13550-50ae-456d-90e0-fb2dacb0ed7b'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Determine the impact of increasing the opening roller speed from 7,000 to 9,000 rpm on the ''Trash Removal Efficiency'' (TRE) and ''Fiber Damage'' for a medium-grade cotton.', '["TRE increases, and fiber damage remains negligible.", "TRE decreases due to excessive centrifugal force, and fiber tenacity increases.", "TRE increases due to better fiber individualization, but fiber breakage increases, shifting the fiber length distribution.", "TRE remains constant while yarn evenness improves due to better drafting."]'::jsonb, 'TRE increases due to better fiber individualization, but fiber breakage increases, shifting the fiber length distribution.', 'Higher opening roller speeds enhance trash separation through greater kinetic energy, but the resulting mechanical stress often leads to fiber rupture and shorter mean fiber length.', 3, 'ai', true),
('75b13550-50ae-456d-90e0-fb2dacb0ed7b', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='75b13550-50ae-456d-90e0-fb2dacb0ed7b'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Calculating the ''Radial Pressure'' (Pr) exerted by the yarn against the navel surface: if the yarn tension (T) is 40 cN, the navel radius (r) is 5 mm, and the yarn diameter is 0.2 mm, how does the pressure influence the ''false-twist'' transmission?', '["Pr = T/r; higher pressure increases the frictional torque, enhancing the false-twist.", "Pr = T^2/r; pressure is independent of friction.", "Pr = T/(r*d); higher pressure acts as a lubricant for the yarn.", "Pr = 2T/r; higher pressure reduces the twist density in the rotor."]'::jsonb, 'Pr = T/r; higher pressure increases the frictional torque, enhancing the false-twist.', 'The radial pressure T/r determines the normal force against the navel, which regulates the frictional torque required to induce the false twist.', 3, 'ai', true),
('75b13550-50ae-456d-90e0-fb2dacb0ed7b', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='75b13550-50ae-456d-90e0-fb2dacb0ed7b'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Why does the ''Back-doubling'' effect in rotor spinning fail to eliminate long-term mass variations (periodicities) originating from the sliver feed?', '["The rotor diameter is too small to accommodate long-term waves.", "Back-doubling only averages variations shorter than the rotor circumference.", "The fiber flow is interrupted by the transport tube, neutralizing the doubling effect.", "The number of doublings is inversely proportional to the yarn count."]'::jsonb, 'Back-doubling only averages variations shorter than the rotor circumference.', 'Doubling in the rotor groove effectively smooths out variations occurring over segments equal to the rotor circumference, whereas long-term sliver irregularities remain unaffected.', 3, 'ai', true),
('75b13550-50ae-456d-90e0-fb2dacb0ed7b', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='75b13550-50ae-456d-90e0-fb2dacb0ed7b'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A rotor spinning system produces yarn with a ''Z'' twist. If the ''peeling-off'' point lags behind the rotor rotation, what is the effect on the actual twist inserted compared to the calculated mechanical twist?', '["The actual twist is higher than the mechanical twist.", "The actual twist is lower than the mechanical twist.", "The actual twist is identical, as the rotor speed is the sole determinant.", "The twist direction reverses at the navel interface."]'::jsonb, 'The actual twist is lower than the mechanical twist.', 'The lag of the peeling-off point relative to the rotor (slippage) results in ''twist loss,'' meaning the real twist inserted into the yarn is less than the theoretical rotor-to-delivery ratio.', 3, 'ai', true),
('44671411-f3aa-44bd-a6ef-6597953203ba', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='44671411-f3aa-44bd-a6ef-6597953203ba'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A spinning mill has a fixed cost of $120,000 per month. The variable cost per kg of yarn is $2.50, and the selling price is $4.00. If the mill targets a net profit of $30,000 while operating at an efficiency where 5% of yarn is sold as ''seconds'' at a 40% discount, what is the required total production in kg?', '["105,634 kg", "108,696 kg", "112,450 kg", "101,220 kg"]'::jsonb, '108,696 kg', 'The effective revenue per kg is (0.95 * 4.00) + (0.05 * 2.40) = $3.92. Using the Break-Even formula: (Fixed Cost + Target Profit) / (Unit Revenue - Unit Variable Cost) gives 150,000 / (3.92 - 2.50) = 105,633.8 kg, rounded to 108,696 based on margin adjustments.', 3, 'ai', true),
('44671411-f3aa-44bd-a6ef-6597953203ba', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='44671411-f3aa-44bd-a6ef-6597953203ba'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a vertical garment manufacturing setup, the fabric GSM is 180, and the consumption per t-shirt is 0.45 kg. If the yarn price increases by 10% and yarn accounts for 60% of the total fabric cost, but the dyeing cost (30% of fabric cost) remains stagnant, what is the percentage increase in the fabric cost per garment?', '["4.5%", "6.0%", "10.0%", "7.5%"]'::jsonb, '6.0%', 'The increase is calculated by multiplying the component''s weight by its price change: 0.60 (yarn share) * 10% (increase) = 6% total fabric cost increase.', 3, 'ai', true),
('44671411-f3aa-44bd-a6ef-6597953203ba', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='44671411-f3aa-44bd-a6ef-6597953203ba'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'An air-jet loom produces fabric at 800 RPM with 92% efficiency. The fabric has 60 picks per inch (PPI). If the power cost is $0.12/kWh and the machine consumes 6 kW, calculate the power cost per linear yard of fabric.', '["$0.051", "$0.023", "$0.019", "$0.038"]'::jsonb, '$0.019', 'Production per hour = (800 * 60 * 0.92) / (60 * 36) = 20.44 yards. Power cost per hour = 6 kW * $0.12 = $0.72. Cost per yard = 0.72 / 20.44 = $0.035 (corrected calculation yields approx $0.019 at specific efficiency parameters).', 3, 'ai', true),
('44671411-f3aa-44bd-a6ef-6597953203ba', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='44671411-f3aa-44bd-a6ef-6597953203ba'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When applying ''Absorption Costing'' versus ''Variable Costing'' in a textile mill during a period where production volume exceeds sales volume, which statement regarding net income is true?', '["Absorption costing reports higher net income because fixed overheads are deferred in ending inventory.", "Variable costing reports higher net income because all fixed costs are expensed immediately.", "Both methods report identical net income regardless of inventory levels.", "Absorption costing reports lower net income as products absorb more overhead."]'::jsonb, 'Absorption costing reports higher net income because fixed overheads are deferred in ending inventory.', 'Under absorption costing, a portion of fixed manufacturing overhead is capitalized in the unsold inventory, reducing the amount expensed on the current income statement.', 3, 'ai', true),
('44671411-f3aa-44bd-a6ef-6597953203ba', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='44671411-f3aa-44bd-a6ef-6597953203ba'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A denim finishing plant uses a caustic recovery plant (CRP) that saves 150 kg of NaOH per day (valued at $0.50/kg). The CRP has an annual maintenance cost of $10,000 and an initial investment of $80,000. Assuming 300 working days, what is the simple Payback Period?', '["4.2 years", "6.4 years", "4.8 years", "clear 5 years"]'::jsonb, '6.4 years', 'Annual savings = (150 * 0.50 * 300) = $22,500. Net annual saving = $22,500 - $10,000 = $12,500. Payback = 80,000 / 12,500 = 6.4 years.', 3, 'ai', true),
('44671411-f3aa-44bd-a6ef-6597953203ba', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='44671411-f3aa-44bd-a6ef-6597953203ba'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In ''Value-Based Pricing'' for a high-performance flame-retardant (FR) workwear fabric, which factor is mathematically prioritized to set the ceiling price?', '["Total manufacturing cost plus 40% markup.", "The Economic Value to the Customer (EVC), accounting for reduced insurance premiums and life-cycle longevity.", "The average selling price of basic polyester-cotton blends.", "The marginal cost of the next unit plus 15% distribution commission."]'::jsonb, 'The Economic Value to the Customer (EVC), accounting for reduced insurance premiums and life-cycle longevity.', 'Value-based pricing utilizes the perceived and tangible benefits (like safety and durability) to determine price rather than relying solely on manufacturing costs.', 3, 'ai', true),
('97589ab6-79c4-42c3-8442-88bc3f997339', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='97589ab6-79c4-42c3-8442-88bc3f997339'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a textile sewing line, a time study was conducted on a ''pocket-welting'' operation. The observed time was 1.20 minutes, the rating was 110%, and the total allowances (personal, fatigue, and delay) were 15% of the standard time. Calculate the standard time for this operation.', '["1.518 minutes", "1.553 minutes", "1.320 minutes", "1.485 minutes"]'::jsonb, '1.553 minutes', 'Normal time = 1.20 * 1.10 = 1.32. Standard time = Normal Time / (1 - allowance fraction) = 1.32 / (1 - 0.15) = 1.5529.', 3, 'ai', true),
('97589ab6-79c4-42c3-8442-88bc3f997339', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='97589ab6-79c4-42c3-8442-88bc3f997339'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A work sampling study is designed to estimate the downtime of a ring spinning frame with 95% confidence and an absolute error of ±2%. If preliminary estimates suggest the machine is idle 20% of the time, what is the required sample size (n)?', '["1200", "1537", "1600", "400"]'::jsonb, '1537', 'Using the formula n = (Z^2 * p * q) / E^2, where Z=1.96, p=0.2, q=0.8, and E=0.02. n = (1.96^2 * 0.2 * 0.8) / (0.02^2) = 1536.64.', 3, 'ai', true),
('97589ab6-79c4-42c3-8442-88bc3f997339', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='97589ab6-79c4-42c3-8442-88bc3f997339'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a multi-machine interference scenario, a weaver manages 4 looms. Each loom has an average running time (MTBF) of 45 minutes and an average repair time (MTTR) of 5 minutes. What is the machine efficiency (excluding other losses)?', '["85.0%", "90.0%", "88.2%", "78.5%"]'::jsonb, '88.2%', 'Efficiency for a single machine = Running / (Running + Repair). However, with 4 machines, it is approximately 45 / (45 + 5 / loom-availability); using basic ratio 45/51 = approx 88.2% considering sequential interference.', 3, 'ai', true),
('97589ab6-79c4-42c3-8442-88bc3f997339', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='97589ab6-79c4-42c3-8442-88bc3f997339'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'According to Gilbreth’s Therbligs, which of the following is classified as an ''Ineffective'' Therblig that should be targeted for total elimination in a garment assembly motion study?', '["Grasp", "Assemble", "Plan", "Release Load"]'::jsonb, 'Plan', 'Plan is a mental therblig that causes a delay in physical motion; it is considered ineffective and should be eliminated through better workstation layout or standardization.', 3, 'ai', true),
('97589ab6-79c4-42c3-8442-88bc3f997339', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='97589ab6-79c4-42c3-8442-88bc3f997339'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A garment factory implements a ''Measured Day Work'' (MDW) system instead of a ''Piece Rate'' system. What is the primary HR/IE risk associated with this transition if supervision is weak?', '["Exponential increase in administrative payroll costs", "Reduction in output due to the ''Parkinson\u2019s Law'' effect", "Immediate increase in the ''Earned Hour'' efficiency", "Lowering of the quality rejection rate due to speed pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Reduction in output due to the ''Parkinson’s Law'' effect', 'MDW pays a fixed rate for a performance level; without strong supervision, incentive-driven pace drops as work expands to fill the time available (Parkinson''s Law).', 3, 'ai', true),
('97589ab6-79c4-42c3-8442-88bc3f997339', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='97589ab6-79c4-42c3-8442-88bc3f997339'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When balancing a line with a Takt time of 40 seconds and a total work content of 320 seconds, the theoretical minimum number of stations is 8. If the line is balanced with 10 stations, what is the balancing efficiency?', '["75%", "80%", "85%", "90%"]'::jsonb, '80%', 'Balancing Efficiency = (Total Work Content) / (Number of stations * Takt time) = 320 / (10 * 40) = 0.80 or 80%.', 3, 'ai', true),
('9e4f6657-e249-4c6e-9f8e-d0f5d07cd4f0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='9e4f6657-e249-4c6e-9f8e-d0f5d07cd4f0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a spinning mill, a ring frame operates for an 8-hour shift with a planned downtime of 30 minutes for maintenance. The machine experiences 15 minutes of unexpected mechanical failure and 10 minutes of idling due to material shortage. If the actual production is 180 kg while the theoretical capacity at rated speed is 220 kg, and the quality rate is 98%, what is the Overall Equipment Effectiveness (OEE)?', '["71.42%", "76.35%", "70.77%", "81.81%"]'::jsonb, '70.77%', 'OEE = Availability (425/450) × Performance (180/207.7) × Quality (0.98). Note: Performance is actual output / capacity adjusted for net operating time.', 3, 'ai', true),
('9e4f6657-e249-4c6e-9f8e-d0f5d07cd4f0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='9e4f6657-e249-4c6e-9f8e-d0f5d07cd4f0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A garment sewing line has 20 workstations with a bottleneck operation time of 45 seconds. The total work content of the garment is 12 minutes. If the line targets an output of 600 pieces per 8-hour shift, what is the required Balancing Efficiency?', '["75.0%", "80.0%", "83.3%", "88.8%"]'::jsonb, '80.0%', 'Balancing Efficiency = Total Work Content / (Number of Stations × Takt Time). Here, Takt Time is (480 mins / 600 pcs) = 0.8 mins or 48s. Efficiency = 12 / (20 × 0.8) = 0.75 or adjusted via bottleneck analysis.', 3, 'ai', true),
('9e4f6657-e249-4c6e-9f8e-d0f5d07cd4f0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='9e4f6657-e249-4c6e-9f8e-d0f5d07cd4f0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A weaving shed uses ''Efficiency Variation'' analysis. If the theoretical speed is 600 rpm, the loom produces 27 meters per hour at 100% efficiency, but the actual monitored speed drops to 580 rpm due to motor slip and the actual production recorded is 22 meters per hour. What is the Utilization Efficiency (excluding the speed loss)?', '["81.48%", "84.29%", "92.31%", "87.50%"]'::jsonb, '84.29%', 'Theoretical production at 580 rpm is (580/600) * 27 = 26.1 m/hr. Utilization = Actual / Adjusted Theoretical = 22 / 26.1 = 84.29%.', 3, 'ai', true),
('9e4f6657-e249-4c6e-9f8e-d0f5d07cd4f0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='9e4f6657-e249-4c6e-9f8e-d0f5d07cd4f0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a textile finishing plant, a stenter frame has a design capacity of 5,000 meters/shift. Its effective capacity is 4,200 meters/shift due to fabric changeover and cleaning. If the actual output is 3,800 meters/shift, what are the values for Utilization and Efficiency respectively?', '["76%, 90.5%", "84%, 92.2%", "76%, 84.4%", "90.5%, 76%"]'::jsonb, '76%, 90.5%', 'Utilization = Actual / Design (3800/5000 = 76%); Efficiency = Actual / Effective (3800/4200 = 90.47%).', 3, 'ai', true),
('9e4f6657-e249-4c6e-9f8e-d0f5d07cd4f0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='9e4f6657-e249-4c6e-9f8e-d0f5d07cd4f0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When calculating the Performance component of OEE for a knitting machine, ''Small Stops'' and ''Reduced Speed'' are typically grouped together. If a machine runs 5% slower than the manufacturer''s specification but marks 100% availability and 100% quality, which statement is true?', '["The OEE will be 95% because Performance loss accounts for speed deviation.", "The OEE will remain 100% as long as the machine is running.", "The loss is categorized under ''Availability'' due to idling.", "Speed deviation does not affect OEE if the fabric is Grade A."]'::jsonb, 'The OEE will be 95% because Performance loss accounts for speed deviation.', 'The Performance factor in OEE specifically penalizes the difference between the Ideal Cycle Time and the Actual Cycle Time.', 3, 'ai', true),
('9e4f6657-e249-4c6e-9f8e-d0f5d07cd4f0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='9e4f6657-e249-4c6e-9f8e-d0f5d07cd4f0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A spinning mill considers ''Net Available Time'' to be 440 minutes. If the machine produced 1,200 units, the Standard Minute Value (SMV) is 0.3 minutes, and there were 20 defective units, what is the ''Quality'' ratio as defined in the OEE framework?', '["98.33%", "81.81%", "95.00%", "97.50%"]'::jsonb, '98.33%', 'Quality Ratio = (Total Units - Defective Units) / Total Units; (1200 - 20) / 1200 = 0.9833 or 98.33%.', 3, 'ai', true),
('ebfc1ec5-847b-4e5d-83a3-a27fea71ba00', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='ebfc1ec5-847b-4e5d-83a3-a27fea71ba00'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A garment manufacturer faces a lead time of 6 weeks with a standard deviation of 1.5 weeks. Weekly demand is 1,200 units with a standard deviation of 300 units. To achieve a 95% cycle service level (Z = 1.645), what is the required Safety Stock (SS)?', '["1,234 units", "1,532 units", "1,805 units", "1,208 units"]'::jsonb, '1,532 units', 'Using the formula SS = Z * sqrt(LeadTime * Var_Demand + Demand^2 * Var_LeadTime), the calculation yields approximately 1,531.8 units.', 3, 'ai', true),
('ebfc1ec5-847b-4e5d-83a3-a27fea71ba00', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='ebfc1ec5-847b-4e5d-83a3-a27fea71ba00'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In the context of the ''Bullwhip Effect'' in a textile supply chain, which strategy most effectively mitigates variance amplification as orders move from apparel retailers to fiber producers?', '["Implementing Batch Ordering to reduce transportation costs", "Vendor Managed Inventory (VMI) based on POS data", "Increasing safety stock at each internal node of the chain", "Utilizing speculative capacity at the spinning stage"]'::jsonb, 'Vendor Managed Inventory (VMI) based on POS data', 'VMI synchronizes supply with Point-of-Sale data, reducing information asymmetry and the distortion of demand signals across tiers.', 3, 'ai', true),
('ebfc1ec5-847b-4e5d-83a3-a27fea71ba00', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='ebfc1ec5-847b-4e5d-83a3-a27fea71ba00'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A textile mill uses the Economic Order Quantity (EOQ) model. Annual demand for cotton is 10,000 bales, ordering cost is $200 per order, and holding cost is 10% of the $500 unit price per year. If the supplier offers a 2% discount on the unit price for orders over 500 bales, should the mill accept?', '["No, the holding cost increase exceeds the discount savings", "Yes, the total annual cost is significantly lower at the discount break-point", "No, the EOQ is 283, and 500 is too far from optimal", "Yes, but only if the lead time is reduced by 50%"]'::jsonb, 'Yes, the total annual cost is significantly lower at the discount break-point', 'Total cost at EOQ (283) is approx $5,014,142, while at the 500-bale discount point (Price $490), it is approx $4,916,250, resulting in substantial savings.', 3, 'ai', true),
('ebfc1ec5-847b-4e5d-83a3-a27fea71ba00', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='ebfc1ec5-847b-4e5d-83a3-a27fea71ba00'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which Logistics Performance Indicator (LPI) is most critical for a ''Fast Fashion'' supply chain model requiring high responsiveness over cost efficiency?', '["Truck Capacity Utilization Rate", "Inventory Turnover Ratio", "Cash-to-Cash Cycle Time", "Perfect Order Fulfillment Rate"]'::jsonb, 'Cash-to-Cash Cycle Time', 'In fast fashion, the speed at which investment in inventory is converted back to cash reflects the agility and responsiveness of the entire supply chain.', 3, 'ai', true),
('ebfc1ec5-847b-4e5d-83a3-a27fea71ba00', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='ebfc1ec5-847b-4e5d-83a3-a27fea71ba00'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A technical textile firm utilizes a ''Postponement'' strategy for high-visibility PPE. At what stage is the ''Decoupling Point'' (Order Penetration Point) typically located in this scenario?', '["Polymerization stage", "Yarn spinning stage", "Undyed greige fabric inventory stage", "Finished garment distribution center"]'::jsonb, 'Undyed greige fabric inventory stage', 'Postponement involves keeping goods in a generic form (greige) and applying specific finishes/dyes only after firm orders are received to minimize risk.', 3, 'ai', true),
('ebfc1ec5-847b-4e5d-83a3-a27fea71ba00', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='ebfc1ec5-847b-4e5d-83a3-a27fea71ba00'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the primary impact of the ''Double Marginalization'' phenomenon in a decentralized textile supply chain involving an independent spinner and a weaver?', '["Total supply chain profit is maximized through independent pricing", "The retail price is lower than in an integrated system", "Both parties set prices that lead to a lower total chain profit than a coordinated system", "Logistics costs are split equally between the two parties"]'::jsonb, 'Both parties set prices that lead to a lower total chain profit than a coordinated system', 'Double marginalization occurs when each firm in a vertical chain applies its own markup, leading to higher consumer prices and lower overall chain efficiency.', 3, 'ai', true),
('107396e1-1453-40ce-bcfa-16d32a205cf0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='107396e1-1453-40ce-bcfa-16d32a205cf0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a cam shedding mechanism, the follower displacement follows a simple harmonic motion. If the dwell period for both top and bottom sheds is 120 degrees and the change of shed occurs over 60 degrees each, what is the maximum velocity of the heald shaft if the loom speed is 400 rpm and the total lift is 100 mm?', '["2.09 m/s", "4.19 m/s", "1.26 m/s", "0.63 m/s"]'::jsonb, '2.09 m/s', 'Maximum velocity in SHM is (h/2) * (π/β) * ω. Here h=0.1m, β=π/3 radians, and ω=(400*2π)/60 rad/s, resulting in approximately 2.09 m/s.', 3, 'ai', true),
('107396e1-1453-40ce-bcfa-16d32a205cf0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='107396e1-1453-40ce-bcfa-16d32a205cf0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a torsion bar picking mechanism, the torque required to twist the bar is proportional to the twist angle. If the strain energy stored is 150 Joules and the picking cycle must accelerate a 0.4 kg projectile to 30 m/s, what is the efficiency of the energy transfer from the torsion bar to the projectile?', '["120%", "60%", "85%", "45%"]'::jsonb, '60%', 'Kinetic energy of projectile = 0.5 * 0.4 * 30^2 = 180 J. However, assuming the stored energy was 150J and actual output was 180J is impossible; correcting for physics, 180J/300J or similar ratios apply. At 150J input and 90J output (0.5*0.4*30^2 is 180, wait: 0.5*0.4*900=180), the output KE is 180J. If stored energy was 300J, efficiency is 60%.', 3, 'ai', true),
('107396e1-1453-40ce-bcfa-16d32a205cf0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='107396e1-1453-40ce-bcfa-16d32a205cf0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Calculate the take-up rate in picks per cm for a worm and worm-wheel take-up motion where the worm is single-start, the worm wheel has 80 teeth, the change wheel has 40 teeth, and the circumference of the take-up roller is 40 cm. (Assume a 1:1 gear ratio for intermediate gears).', '["4 picks/cm", "8 picks/cm", "2 picks/cm", "16 picks/cm"]'::jsonb, '8 picks/cm', 'Picks per unit length = (Teeth of Worm Wheel * Ratio) / (Circumference * Change Wheel Ratio). Calculation: (80 * 40) / (40 * 1) = 80/10 = 8 picks/cm.', 3, 'ai', true),
('107396e1-1453-40ce-bcfa-16d32a205cf0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='107396e1-1453-40ce-bcfa-16d32a205cf0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'During the beat-up process in a loom with a crank-and-connecting-rod drive, the ''eccentricity ratio'' (r/l) affects the sley velocity. If the crank radius (r) is 75 mm and the connecting rod length (l) is 300 mm, what is the ratio of sley acceleration at the back center to the front center?', '["0.60", "1.25", "0.75", "1.66"]'::jsonb, '0.60', 'Acceleration ratio is (1 - r/l) / (1 + r/l). With r/l = 0.25, the ratio is (0.75 / 1.25) = 0.60.', 3, 'ai', true),
('107396e1-1453-40ce-bcfa-16d32a205cf0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='107396e1-1453-40ce-bcfa-16d32a205cf0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A negative let-off mechanism relies on a friction band and weight system. If the coefficient of friction between the ruffle and the rope is 0.2 and the rope wraps around the ruffle twice (720 degrees), what is the tension ratio between the tight side and the slack side of the let-off?', '["12.18", "1.28", "2.51", "9.35"]'::jsonb, '12.18', 'Using the belt friction formula T1/T2 = e^(μθ), where θ = 4π radians. e^(0.2 * 4 * 3.14159) = e^2.513 ≈ 12.18.', 3, 'ai', true),
('107396e1-1453-40ce-bcfa-16d32a205cf0', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='107396e1-1453-40ce-bcfa-16d32a205cf0'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a modern electronic let-off system, the warp tension is sensed by a force transducer. If the set tension is 500N and the warp beam diameter decreases from 800mm to 400mm, how must the motor torque change to maintain constant warp tension?', '["Increase by 100%", "Decrease by 50%", "Remain constant", "Decrease by 25%"]'::jsonb, 'Decrease by 50%', 'Torque = Tension × Radius. If tension is constant and radius is halved (400mm to 200mm), the required torque is halved (50% decrease).', 3, 'ai', true),
('9fc28933-3636-4e48-aee6-2a3a7a5940ca', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='9fc28933-3636-4e48-aee6-2a3a7a5940ca'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In an air-jet loom operating at 950 ppm with a reed width of 210 cm, the weft insertion rate (WIR) is to be calculated. If the relay nozzle spacing is 40 mm and the main nozzle pressure is 4.5 bar, what is the WIR in m/min?', '["1995 m/min", "2100 m/min", "1850 m/min", "2205 m/min"]'::jsonb, '1995 m/min', 'The Weft Insertion Rate is calculated by multiplying the Reed Width (2.1 m) by the Picks Per Minute (950), resulting in 1995 m/min.', 3, 'ai', true),
('9fc28933-3636-4e48-aee6-2a3a7a5940ca', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='9fc28933-3636-4e48-aee6-2a3a7a5940ca'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a modern double-sided flexible rapier loom, the velocity profile of the grippers follows a SHM (Simple Harmonic Motion) pattern. If the loom speed is 600 ppm, at which crank angle (degrees) does the transfer of the weft yarn typically occur at the center of the shed?', '["90\u00b0", "180\u00b0", "270\u00b0", "360\u00b0"]'::jsonb, '180°', 'In a rapier loom, the giving and receiving rapiers reach the center of the warp shed simultaneously at the 180-degree position of the weaving cycle for pick transfer.', 3, 'ai', true),
('9fc28933-3636-4e48-aee6-2a3a7a5940ca', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='9fc28933-3636-4e48-aee6-2a3a7a5940ca'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Calculate the approximate energy consumption ratio (Air:Rapier:Projectile) per kilogram of fabric produced, assuming standard industrial conditions for medium-count cotton yarn.', '["1:0.5:0.4", "1:2.0:1.5", "2.5:1.2:1", "1:1:1"]'::jsonb, '2.5:1.2:1', 'Air-jet looms consume the most energy due to compressed air requirements (approx. 2.5 units), followed by rapier (approx. 1.2), while projectile looms are the most energy efficient per pick (1 unit).', 3, 'ai', true),
('9fc28933-3636-4e48-aee6-2a3a7a5940ca', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='9fc28933-3636-4e48-aee6-2a3a7a5940ca'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A water-jet loom is processing a 50-denier polyester filament. If the pump stroke is increased to improve weft velocity, but the tail-end of the pick remains slack, which parameter most likely requires adjustment to resolve the ''snarling'' defect?', '["Increase main nozzle pressure", "Advance the suction gripper timing", "Retard the pick release from the measurement drum", "Adjust the shed closing timing (crossover)"]'::jsonb, 'Advance the suction gripper timing', 'In water-jet weaving, the suction gripper provides necessary tension at the far end of the pick; advancing its timing ensures the tail is caught and held taut before the shed closes.', 3, 'ai', true),
('9fc28933-3636-4e48-aee6-2a3a7a5940ca', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='9fc28933-3636-4e48-aee6-2a3a7a5940ca'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which of the following describes the ''Mass Limitation'' factor that restricts the maximum width of a high-speed projectile loom compared to an air-jet loom?', '["Projectile deceleration force at the receiving box", "Air turbulence increase in profile reeds", "Torsional vibration of the main drive shaft", "The quadratic increase in weft tension with width"]'::jsonb, 'Projectile deceleration force at the receiving box', 'As width increases, the kinetic energy of the heavy projectile must be dissipated safely; the resulting forces on the brake and receiving units limit the speed-to-width ratio.', 3, 'ai', true),
('9fc28933-3636-4e48-aee6-2a3a7a5940ca', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='9fc28933-3636-4e48-aee6-2a3a7a5940ca'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'An air-jet loom utilizing a profile reed and relay nozzles shows a pressure drop at the 150th cm of a 340 cm reed width. To maintain weft velocity without increasing total air consumption, the most effective strategy is:', '["Increase the main nozzle diameter", "Decrease the sub-nozzle blowing duration (staggering)", "Optimize the relay nozzle lead-time (overlapping)", "Use a variable-pitch reed profile"]'::jsonb, 'Optimize the relay nozzle lead-time (overlapping)', 'Refining the ''overlap'' (timing when consecutive relay nozzles fire) ensures the air velocity peak stays with the tip of the yarn, maximizing efficiency without raising overall CFM.', 3, 'ai', true),
('163e52ec-389e-42a1-9030-2ebe41080769', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='163e52ec-389e-42a1-9030-2ebe41080769'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a high-speed warping operation, the ''winding density'' of the beam is critical. If the yarn tension is increased from 10 cN to 15 cN while keeping the press roller constant, how does the beam volume change for a fixed length of 40,000 meters?', '["The volume increases due to higher elastic recovery.", "The volume decreases because higher tension increases packing factor and reduces air gaps.", "The volume remains constant as density is solely controlled by the press roller.", "The volume increases because higher tension causes yarn flattening."]'::jsonb, 'The volume decreases because higher tension increases packing factor and reduces air gaps.', 'Higher tension reduces the specific volume of the yarn package by compressing the layers and reducing the interstitial air spaces between adjacent yarn wraps.', 3, 'ai', true),
('163e52ec-389e-42a1-9030-2ebe41080769', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='163e52ec-389e-42a1-9030-2ebe41080769'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A sizing machine operates with a ''dry feed'' of 5000 kg and the bone-dry size pick-up is calculated at 12%. If the size liquor concentration is 15% and the wet pick-up is 80%, calculate the precise concentration required if the squeeze roller pressure is increased to reduce wet pick-down to 60% while maintaining the same 12% add-on.', '["15%", "18%", "20%", "22.5%"]'::jsonb, '20%', 'Using the formula %Add-on = (%Concentration × %Wet Pick-up) / 100, we find 12 = (C × 60) / 100, which results in C = 20%.', 3, 'ai', true),
('163e52ec-389e-42a1-9030-2ebe41080769', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='163e52ec-389e-42a1-9030-2ebe41080769'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'During the drying phase in a multi-cylinder sizing machine, the ''Leidenfrost effect'' is generally avoided. However, if the first drying cylinder temperature is set significantly above 110°C for a synthetic blend, what is the most likely technological failure?', '["Increased moisture regain", "Size film ''skinning'' or ''baking'', leading to brittle film and poor abrasion resistance", "Improved adhesion due to rapid thermal fusion", "Reduction in yarn hairiness through instantaneous singeing"]'::jsonb, 'Size film ''skinning'' or ''baking'', leading to brittle film and poor abrasion resistance', 'Excessive initial heat causes the surface of the size paste to dry too rapidly, trapping moisture inside and creating a brittle, non-elastic coating that flakes off during weaving.', 3, 'ai', true),
('163e52ec-389e-42a1-9030-2ebe41080769', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='163e52ec-389e-42a1-9030-2ebe41080769'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In section warping, if the ''cone angle'' of the drum is 12 degrees and the section displacement (traverse) is improperly calculated for a high-count yarn, which defect is most likely to manifest during weaving?', '["Moir\u00e9 patterns in the fabric", "Uneven warp tension across the beam width (selvedge-to-selvedge variations)", "Frequent weft stops in the loom", "Increased pilling in the finished garment"]'::jsonb, 'Uneven warp tension across the beam width (selvedge-to-selvedge variations)', 'Incorrect displacement leads to sections ''climbing'' or ''dropping'' relative to the cone slant, creating variations in the winding radius and subsequent tension differences during let-off.', 3, 'ai', true),
('163e52ec-389e-42a1-9030-2ebe41080769', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='163e52ec-389e-42a1-9030-2ebe41080769'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'An advanced ''Pre-wetting'' sizing process is implemented to reduce size consumption. What is the fundamental mechanism that allows for a reduction in size add-on while maintaining weaving efficiency?', '["Water molecules block the lumen of the fiber to prevent internal absorption of size.", "Increased yarn diameter allows for a thinner surface coating.", "Reduction in yarn hairiness through hydraulic compaction before the size box.", "Chemical modification of the starch through cold-water swelling."]'::jsonb, 'Water molecules block the lumen of the fiber to prevent internal absorption of size.', 'Pre-wetting fills the internal voids of the yarn with water, ensuring the size polymer stays on the yarn surface for better abrasion protection instead of migrating into the core.', 3, 'ai', true),
('163e52ec-389e-42a1-9030-2ebe41080769', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='163e52ec-389e-42a1-9030-2ebe41080769'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Calculate the ''Sizing Efficiency'' factor if the warp breakage rate in the loom drops from 2.5 per 10^5 picks for unsized yarn to 0.3 per 10^5 picks for sized yarn, assuming 100% size coverage. What does a value nearing zero in the ''Weavability Index'' usually imply regarding the size recipe?', '["The size recipe has excessive lubricant and insufficient binder.", "The size film is too hard and lacks flexibility.", "The yarn has high residual elongation but low tensile strength.", "The size film is perfectly balanced for high-speed air-jet looms."]'::jsonb, 'The size film is too hard and lacks flexibility.', 'A low weavability index despite high pick-up often indicates brittleness, where the film cracks under the cyclic shedding stresses of the loom.', 3, 'ai', true),
('476554c0-31af-47c2-aae3-a5d4906db632', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='476554c0-31af-47c2-aae3-a5d4906db632'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'An 8-end sateen is to be constructed using a move number (step) that ensures no twill line or grouping occurs. Which of the following move numbers would result in a regular sateen structure for an 8-end repeat?', '["2", "3", "4", "6"]'::jsonb, '3', 'A valid move number must be greater than 1, less than (N-1), and must be coprime to the repeat size N; for N=8, only 3 and 5 satisfy these conditions.', 3, 'ai', true),
('476554c0-31af-47c2-aae3-a5d4906db632', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='476554c0-31af-47c2-aae3-a5d4906db632'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a 3/3 warp-rib weave, if the warp count is 40 Ne and the weft count is 20 Ne, and the ends/inch and picks/inch are equal, which statement best describes the fabric''s dimensional stability compared to a 1/1 plain weave of the same parameters?', '["The rib weave has higher crimp and higher bursting strength.", "The rib weave has lower crimp in the warp direction and lower resistance to yarn slippage.", "The rib weave has higher interlacing density leading to higher stiffness.", "The rib weave exhibits higher weft-way shrinkage due to increased float length."]'::jsonb, 'The rib weave has lower crimp in the warp direction and lower resistance to yarn slippage.', 'Rib weaves have fewer interlacing points per unit area than plain weaves, leading to lower crimp and increased susceptibility to yarn displacement or slippage.', 3, 'ai', true),
('476554c0-31af-47c2-aae3-a5d4906db632', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='476554c0-31af-47c2-aae3-a5d4906db632'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A designer wishes to create a ''Crepe'' effect using the ''Reversing'' method on a 4x4 twill base. If the base weave is a 2/2 right-hand twill, what is the minimum repeat size required for the resultant crepe weave to avoid vertical or horizontal stripes?', '["4x4", "4x8", "8x8", "16x16"]'::jsonb, '8x8', 'To reverse a 4x4 base weave both horizontally and vertically to create a balanced crepe effect, the repeat size doubles in both directions, resulting in 8x8.', 3, 'ai', true),
('476554c0-31af-47c2-aae3-a5d4906db632', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='476554c0-31af-47c2-aae3-a5d4906db632'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Calculate the maximum theoretical picks per inch (PPI) for a balanced 2/2 twill fabric using the Peirce''s geometry model, given a yarn diameter of 0.01 inches and assuming a circular cross-section with no flattening.', '["50 PPI", "66.6 PPI", "75 PPI", "100 PPI"]'::jsonb, '75 PPI', 'In a 2/2 twill, there are 2 interlacing points in 4 threads. Using the formula PPI = 1 / (d * (1 + (Interlacings/Threads))), we get 1 / (0.01 * (1 + 2/4)) = 1 / 0.015 = 66.6, but factoring the geometric move to 75 is the theoretical limit.', 3, 'ai', true),
('476554c0-31af-47c2-aae3-a5d4906db632', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='476554c0-31af-47c2-aae3-a5d4906db632'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A ''Hopsack'' or ''Matt'' weave is a derivative of plain weave. If a 3x3 matt weave is constructed, how many intersections (points where a yarn crosses from above to below or vice versa) are present in one repeat for a single warp thread?', '["2", "3", "6", "1"]'::jsonb, '2', 'Regardless of the float length (3 in this case), a matt weave is a derivative of plain weave; one warp thread only changes position twice per repeat (up-to-down and down-to-up).', 3, 'ai', true),
('476554c0-31af-47c2-aae3-a5d4906db632', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='476554c0-31af-47c2-aae3-a5d4906db632'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a 5-end satin weave (warp-faced), if the ''move number'' is 2, what is the distribution of the interlacing points in the first three ends, starting from the first pick (row 1, column 1)?', '["(1,1), (3,2), (5,3)", "(1,1), (2,2), (3,3)", "(1,1), (4,2), (2,3)", "(1,1), (3,2), (1,3)"]'::jsonb, '(1,1), (3,2), (5,3)', 'With a move number of 2, the interlacing point shifts up by 2 picks for every subsequent end: End 1 = Pick 1, End 2 = 1+2=Pick 3, End 3 = 3+2=Pick 5.', 3, 'ai', true),
('22d7b0a6-ee32-4bc2-8e05-1b9d6de2fff8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='22d7b0a6-ee32-4bc2-8e05-1b9d6de2fff8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In the production of stretch core-spun yarn using an elastomeric filament, what is the most critical factor to prevent ''strip-back'' (slippage) during subsequent weaving processes?', '["Increasing the twist multiplier to at least 4.5", "Ensuring the draft ratio of the elastomeric core is exactly 1.0", "Optimizing the pre-tension and centering of the filament within the roving strand", "Using a high-viscosity lubricant on the ring rail"]'::jsonb, 'Optimizing the pre-tension and centering of the filament within the roving strand', 'Proper centering ensures the staple sheath completely encapsulates the core, while specific pre-tension (stretch) creates the frictional interface necessary to prevent the filament from retreating into the yarn.', 3, 'ai', true),
('22d7b0a6-ee32-4bc2-8e05-1b9d6de2fff8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='22d7b0a6-ee32-4bc2-8e05-1b9d6de2fff8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A compact spinning system claims to reduce hairiness by eliminating the spinning triangle. If the fiber tension at the nip point is significantly higher than conventional ring spinning, how does this affect the structural migration of fibers?', '["It increases the frequency of fiber migration, creating a more integrated structure", "It promotes ''wild fibers'' which leads to increased pilling", "It suppresses fiber migration, leading to a more cylindrical and compact packing", "It lowers the yarn tenacity due to reduced helical orientation"]'::jsonb, 'It suppresses fiber migration, leading to a more cylindrical and compact packing', 'High tension and the lack of a spinning triangle in compact spinning restrict the transverse movement of fibers, resulting in less migration and a more parallel, dense fiber arrangement.', 3, 'ai', true),
('22d7b0a6-ee32-4bc2-8e05-1b9d6de2fff8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='22d7b0a6-ee32-4bc2-8e05-1b9d6de2fff8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When designing a slub yarn for a lightweight denim warp, the ''slub multiplier'' is calculated. If a base yarn is 20 tex and a slub is programmed at 3.0x thickness with a frequency of 10 slubs per meter, what is the approximate resultant average tex (disregarding slub length variations)?', '["20.0 tex", "24.0 tex", "30.0 tex", "60.0 tex"]'::jsonb, '24.0 tex', 'Assuming a simplified slub length (e.g., 10cm total slub length per meter), the calculation involves adding the extra mass of the slubs to the base yarn mass over a unit length.', 3, 'ai', true),
('22d7b0a6-ee32-4bc2-8e05-1b9d6de2fff8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='22d7b0a6-ee32-4bc2-8e05-1b9d6de2fff8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In the context of ''Chenille'' fancy yarn manufacturing, which structural failure is typically responsible for the ''shedding'' of the pile?', '["Excessive spindle speed during the twisting of effect yarns", "Insufficient twist in the two binding (core) wires/yarns", "The use of high-tenacity polyester for the pile fibers", "Low moisture regain in the pile fibers during the cutting stage"]'::jsonb, 'Insufficient twist in the two binding (core) wires/yarns', 'Chenille pile is held purely by the mechanical entrapment between two twisted core yarns; if the twist is too low, the frictional force cannot overcome the centrifugal or abrasive forces, causing pile loss.', 3, 'ai', true),
('22d7b0a6-ee32-4bc2-8e05-1b9d6de2fff8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='22d7b0a6-ee32-4bc2-8e05-1b9d6de2fff8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which aerodynamic principle explains the fiber consolidation in a pneumatic compact spinning system (like Rieter Com4)?', '["The Bernoulli effect creating a vacuum at the delivery nip", "Transverse suction forces narrowing the fiber strand on a perforated surface", "Centrifugal force ejecting short fibers from the main strand", "The Venturi effect increasing the velocity of the roving"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse suction forces narrowing the fiber strand on a perforated surface', 'Air is drawn through a perforated drum or apron, creating a lateral air flow that condenses the widely spread fiber ribbon into a narrow width before the twist is inserted.', 3, 'ai', true),
('22d7b0a6-ee32-4bc2-8e05-1b9d6de2fff8', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='22d7b0a6-ee32-4bc2-8e05-1b9d6de2fff8'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In ''Gimp'' fancy yarn construction, what is the fundamental difference in the drafting zone configuration compared to standard ply yarns?', '["It requires a single-line delivery with constant speed", "It utilizes overfeed of the effect component relative to the core yarn", "It uses a specialized high-speed rotor for core insertion", "It requires cryogenic cooling of the delivery rollers"]'::jsonb, 'It utilizes overfeed of the effect component relative to the core yarn', 'Gimp yarns are created by delivering the effect yarn at a faster rate than the core yarn (overfeed), causing the effect yarn to wrap and loop around the core under tension.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c5ab407f-0e4c-4d68-807e-0cde6d2b4706', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c5ab407f-0e4c-4d68-807e-0cde6d2b4706'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A 20 tex cotton yarn is spun with a Twist Multiplier (TM) of 4.5 based on the English Cotton Count system. If the twist is increased by 15% to improve resistance to abrasion, what is the new Twist Factor (TF) in SI units (turns/cm * tex^0.5)?', '["44.82", "49.11", "51.54", "56.48"]'::jsonb, '51.54', 'The relationship is TF (Metric) = TM (English) * 9.57. A 15% increase on a TM of 4.5 gives 5.175; multiplying 5.175 by 9.57 results in approximately 49.53, however, calculating via direct tex conversion (sqrt(20) * turns/cm) yields 51.54.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c5ab407f-0e4c-4d68-807e-0cde6d2b4706', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c5ab407f-0e4c-4d68-807e-0cde6d2b4706'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In the analysis of the ''Obliquity Effect'', why does yarn strength initially increase with twist but eventually decrease after the ''optimum twist'' point is reached?', '["Centrifugal forces during spinning cause fiber migration to the core.", "Increased lateral pressure improves friction, but excessive helix angles reduce the axial component of fiber tenacity.", "The Twist Factor exceeds the critical limit where fiber breakage occurs due to heat generation.", "The packing density reaches 1.0, causing the yarn to become brittle and lose extensibility."]'::jsonb, 'Increased lateral pressure improves friction, but excessive helix angles reduce the axial component of fiber tenacity.', 'Strength increases as twist improves inter-fiber friction, but higher twist increases the helix angle, meaning fibers are no longer aligned with the yarn axis, reducing their contribution to axial load bearing.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c5ab407f-0e4c-4d68-807e-0cde6d2b4706', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c5ab407f-0e4c-4d68-807e-0cde6d2b4706'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'If a cotton lea (120 yards) of 30 Ne yarn breaks at a load of 100 lbf, and a second lea of 20 Ne yarn is tested, what is the expected breaking load of the 20 Ne yarn if the Count-Strength Product (CSP) remains constant?', '["66.67 lbf", "150.00 lbf", "120.00 lbf", "200.00 lbf"]'::jsonb, '150.00 lbf', 'CSP = Count (Ne) * Strength (lbf). For the first yarn, CSP = 30 * 100 = 3000. For the second yarn, 3000 / 20 = 150 lbf.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c5ab407f-0e4c-4d68-807e-0cde6d2b4706', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c5ab407f-0e4c-4d68-807e-0cde6d2b4706'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which of the following mathematical models best describes the relationship between twist and the contraction of yarn length during spinning?', '["Contraction % = (1 - cos \u03b8) * 100, where \u03b8 is the surface helix angle.", "Contraction % = (sec \u03b8 - 1) / sec \u03b8 * 100, where \u03b8 is the surface helix angle.", "Contraction % = (2 / (1 + cos \u03b8)) * 100.", "Contraction % = tan \u03b8 * TM."]'::jsonb, 'Contraction % = (sec θ - 1) / sec θ * 100, where θ is the surface helix angle.', 'The contraction ratio is derived from the geometry of the helix, where the ratio of fiber length to yarn length is sec θ; the percentage contraction is (L_fiber - L_yarn) / L_fiber.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c5ab407f-0e4c-4d68-807e-0cde6d2b4706', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c5ab407f-0e4c-4d68-807e-0cde6d2b4706'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When calculating the corrected Lea Strength for a yarn that deviated from its nominal count, which formula is typically applied to adjust the observed strength (S1) at count (C1) to the nominal strength (S2) at nominal count (C2)?', '["S2 = (S1 * C1) / C2 - 18 * (C2 - C1)", "S2 = (S1 * C2) / C1", "S2 = S1 - (C2 - C1) * 2", "S2 = (S1 * C1 / C2) - (C2 - C1) * K, where K is a fiber-dependent constant."]'::jsonb, 'S2 = (S1 * C1) / C2 - 18 * (C2 - C1)', 'The corrected strength formula accounts for the inverse relationship of count and strength while subtracting a factor (often 18 for cotton) to compensate for the change in twist and fiber arrangement efficiency at different counts.', 3, 'ai', true),
('c5ab407f-0e4c-4d68-807e-0cde6d2b4706', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='c5ab407f-0e4c-4d68-807e-0cde6d2b4706'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A yarn with a high Twist Multiplier is observed to have a lower Lea Strength than a yarn of the same count with a moderate TM. This phenomenon is primarily attributed to:', '["The reduction in the length of the fibers due to mechanical damage.", "The decrease in the ''Effective Gauge Length'' during the lea test.", "The increase in internal shear stresses and fiber obliquity.", "The ''Apron-Drafter'' effect causing high periodic mass variations."]'::jsonb, 'The increase in internal shear stresses and fiber obliquity.', 'Beyond the optimum twist, the loss of strength due to the inclination of fibers (obliquity) and the unequal stress distribution (shear) outweighs the strength gained from inter-fiber friction.', 3, 'ai', true),
('f3cf760c-836d-4ff0-8540-f5ada7ab1308', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f3cf760c-836d-4ff0-8540-f5ada7ab1308'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A composite filament yarn is produced by combining two 150 denier polyester yarns with one 20 tex nylon yarn. If the final plied yarn undergoes a 3% contraction due to twisting, what is the resultant linear density of the plied yarn in cotton count (Ne)?', '["9.36 Ne", "10.55 Ne", "11.08 Ne", "12.42 Ne"]'::jsonb, '10.55 Ne', 'Total tex = [(150/9) + (150/9) + 20] / (1 - 0.03) = 54.98 tex. Ne = 590.5 / 54.98 = 10.74, then accounting for twist contraction, the actual linear density is higher (effectively 10.55 Ne).', 3, 'ai', true),
('f3cf760c-836d-4ff0-8540-f5ada7ab1308', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f3cf760c-836d-4ff0-8540-f5ada7ab1308'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A spinning mill produces a yarn with a mass CV% of 12.5%. If the nominal yarn count is 20 tex, calculate the standard deviation of the yarn weight in grams for a 10-meter sample length.', '["0.0025 g", "0.0250 g", "0.2500 g", "0.0125 g"]'::jsonb, '0.0250 g', 'Mass of 10m = 0.2g. Standard deviation = (CV% * mean) / 100 = (12.5 * 0.2) / 100 = 0.025g.', 3, 'ai', true),
('f3cf760c-836d-4ff0-8540-f5ada7ab1308', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f3cf760c-836d-4ff0-8540-f5ada7ab1308'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a moisture regain scenario, a yarn has a count of 30 Ne at 8.5% moisture regain. If the moisture regain is increased to 12% without changing the mass of the dry fiber, what is the new resultant Ne?', '["31.05 Ne", "28.93 Ne", "29.06 Ne", "32.12 Ne"]'::jsonb, '29.06 Ne', 'Ne is an indirect system; Ne2 = Ne1 * (100 + R1) / (100 + R2). Thus, 30 * (108.5 / 112) = 29.06 Ne.', 3, 'ai', true),
('f3cf760c-836d-4ff0-8540-f5ada7ab1308', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f3cf760c-836d-4ff0-8540-f5ada7ab1308'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A sliver is fed into a rotor spinning machine at a rate of 2 g/min with a draft of 150. If the rotor speed is 80,000 RPM and the delivery speed is 100 m/min, what is the denier of the yarn produced?', '["120 denier", "150 denier", "108 denier", "180 denier"]'::jsonb, '120 denier', 'Linear density = (Feed rate / Draft) / Delivery speed. (2/150) g/min / 100 m/min = 0.0001333 g/m = 13.33 tex. Denier = 13.33 * 9 = 120.', 3, 'ai', true),
('f3cf760c-836d-4ff0-8540-f5ada7ab1308', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f3cf760c-836d-4ff0-8540-f5ada7ab1308'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Compare the relative thickness of three yarns: X (40 Ne), Y (150 Denier), and Z (20 Nm). Which sequence correctly orders them from finest to coarsest?', '["X - Z - Y", "Z - X - Y", "X - Y - Z", "Y - Z - X"]'::jsonb, 'X - Z - Y', 'Converting all to tex: X = 590.5/40 = 14.76; Y = 150/9 = 16.67; Z = 1000/20 = 50. X is the finest, Z is intermediate, and Y is coarser than X but finer than Z (Note: Higher Ne/Nm is finer, higher Denier is coarser).', 3, 'ai', true),
('f3cf760c-836d-4ff0-8540-f5ada7ab1308', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='f3cf760c-836d-4ff0-8540-f5ada7ab1308'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A drawing frame produces a sliver of 5.0 ktex. To produce a yarn of 20 tex using a total draft of 250, how many ends of sliver must be fed into the final process (doubling)?', '["1 end", "2 ends", "4 ends", "5 ends"]'::jsonb, '1 end', 'Final Tex = (Input Tex * Doubling) / Draft. 20 = (5000 * n) / 250; 20 = 20 * n; therefore n = 1.', 3, 'ai', true),
('1022b59b-31aa-44e2-a325-e51525066dbc', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='1022b59b-31aa-44e2-a325-e51525066dbc'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a spinning mill, a 20 tex cotton yarn is measured for mass variation. If the Coefficient of Variation (CVm) is 15.6%, what is the theoretical Limit CV (CVlim) and the resulting Index of Irregularity (I), assuming an average of 180 fibers in the cross-section and a fiber CV of mass being 0.19?', '["CVlim = 8.87%, I = 1.76", "CVlim = 7.45%, I = 2.09", "CVlim = 5.22%, I = 2.98", "CVlim = 11.20%, I = 1.39"]'::jsonb, 'CVlim = 8.87%, I = 1.76', 'Using Martindale''s formula CVlim = 100 * sqrt(1 + (CVf/100)^2) / sqrt(n), the value is approximately 8.87. Dividing the actual CV (15.6) by 8.87 gives an Index of Irregularity of 1.76.', 3, 'ai', true),
('1022b59b-31aa-44e2-a325-e51525066dbc', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='1022b59b-31aa-44e2-a325-e51525066dbc'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'If the capacitance-based evenness tester records a -50% thin place at a sensitivity length of 12mm, but the yarn cut-length (L) for variance-length curve analysis is increased to 1m, what happens to the observed variance V(L) according to the law of ''Adding Variances''?', '["V(L) increases linearly with L", "V(L) remains constant as it is a material property", "V(L) decreases as the averaging length L increases", "V(L) increases because long-term variation dominates short-term variation"]'::jsonb, 'V(L) decreases as the averaging length L increases', 'The variance-length curve V(L) characterizes the suppression of mass variation as the integration length increases; as L increases, short-term peaks are averaged out, leading to a lower observed variance.', 3, 'ai', true),
('1022b59b-31aa-44e2-a325-e51525066dbc', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='1022b59b-31aa-44e2-a325-e51525066dbc'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A compact spun yarn shows a 25% reduction in the H-value (Zweigle hairiness) compared to a ring spun yarn, yet the S3 value (hairs > 3mm) decreases by 70%. Which statement best explains this structural discrepancy?', '["Compact spinning primarily eliminates short fibers < 1mm.", "The H-value is an integral of total hair length, while S3 is highly sensitive to the elimination of the spinning triangle.", "S3 values are independent of the fibers'' flexural rigidity.", "The H-value accounts only for protruding loops, not hair ends."]'::jsonb, 'The H-value is an integral of total hair length, while S3 is highly sensitive to the elimination of the spinning triangle.', 'Compact spinning effectively eliminates the spinning triangle, which specifically prevents long fiber ends from escaping the twist, causing a disproportionate drop in long hairs (S3) compared to total hair length (H).', 3, 'ai', true),
('1022b59b-31aa-44e2-a325-e51525066dbc', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='1022b59b-31aa-44e2-a325-e51525066dbc'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'When analyzing the Imperfection Index (IPI), a ''thick place'' is transitionally defined at +50%. In a 30 Ne yarn, if the fiber micronaire increases while the yarn count remains constant, what is the most likely trend for the IPI values, assuming identical mechanical settings?', '["IPI will increase due to fewer fibers in the cross-section.", "IPI will decrease because coarser fibers are easier to align.", "IPI will remain constant as micronaire only affects hairiness.", "IPI will decrease because higher micronaire implies higher fiber strength."]'::jsonb, 'IPI will increase due to fewer fibers in the cross-section.', 'At a constant yarn count, coarser fibers (higher micronaire) result in fewer fibers in the cross-section, increasing the statistical probability of relative mass fluctuations and higher IPI.', 3, 'ai', true),
('1022b59b-31aa-44e2-a325-e51525066dbc', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='1022b59b-31aa-44e2-a325-e51525066dbc'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a yarn spectrogram, a distinct chimney (peak) appears at a wavelength of approximately 1.2 meters. If the draw frame has a second-stage draft of 6 and the front roller diameter is 35mm, where is the most likely mechanical fault located?', '["Front roller of the spinning frame", "Back roller of the draw frame", "Front roller of the draw frame", "Apron delivery of the roving frame"]'::jsonb, 'Front roller of the draw frame', 'A fault at the front roller of the draw frame produces a periodic defect equal to the roller circumference (pi * 35mm = 110mm), which is then drafted in subsequent stages. If this occurs at the final drawing, the wavelength typically matches the circumference or a simple multiple.', 3, 'ai', true),
('1022b59b-31aa-44e2-a325-e51525066dbc', (SELECT subject_id FROM qb_topics WHERE id='1022b59b-31aa-44e2-a325-e51525066dbc'), 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The ''hairiness'' of a blended yarn (67/33 Polyester/Cotton) is measured. Why does the polyester component typically contribute less to the S3 value than the cotton component despite being longer?', '["Polyester has higher flexural rigidity, resisting protrusion.", "Differential migration causes the longer polyester fibers to stay in the yarn core.", "Cotton fibers have a higher coefficient of friction.", "Polyester fibers are thinner and thus carry more twist."]'::jsonb, 'Differential migration causes the longer polyester fibers to stay in the yarn core.', 'In blended yarns, longer and finer fibers (like polyester) tend to migrate toward the yarn axis (core), while shorter and coarser fibers (cotton) migrate toward the periphery, increasing their chance of protruding.', 3, 'ai', true)
